OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026", "title": "A linear list-coloring bound in terms of the Hadwiger number", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "157", "family_title": "Graph coloring, clique minors, and Colin de Verdière invariants", "abstract_html": "We prove that every finite nonempty graph <i>G</i> satisfies \\(\\chi_{\\mathrm{list}}(G)\\le C h(G)\\) for an absolute integer <i>C</i>, where \\(h(G)\\) is the largest order of a clique minor. This resolves the Linear List Hadwiger conjecture affirmatively.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an absolute integer \\(C\\ge1\\) such that every finite nonempty simple graph \\(G\\) satisfies \\( \\ell(G)\\le Ch(G). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All graphs are finite, simple, and undirected.</p><p class=\"li\">A clique model of order \\(t\\) consists of \\(t\\) pairwise disjoint nonempty connected vertex sets, called bags, with an edge between every two bags.</p><p class=\"li\">The Hadwiger number \\(h(G)\\) is the largest order of a clique model in \\(G\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A list assignment \\(\\mathcal L\\) assigns a finite set of colors to each vertex. An \\(\\mathcal L\\)-coloring is a proper coloring \\(c\\) satisfying \\(c(v)\\in\\mathcal L(v)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The list chromatic number \\(\\ell(G)=\\chi_{\\mathrm{list}}(G)\\) is the least integer \\(k\\) such that every list assignment with \\(|\\mathcal L(v)|\\ge k\\) is colorable.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an absolute integer \\(C\\ge1\\) such that every finite nonempty simple graph \\(G\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\ell(G)\\le Ch(G).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(K,Q\\) be the constants in Proposition 11. First, for every integer \\(t\\ge2\\), we prove\n\\[\nh(G)&lt;t\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\ell(G)\\le(Q+6K)t. \\tag{55}\n\\]</p><p>Otherwise Proposition 3, with connectivity parameter \\(Kt\\), gives a \\(Kt\\)-connected induced subgraph \\(P\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\ell(P)\\ge\\ell(G)-6Kt&gt;Qt.\n\\]\nProposition 11 makes \\(P\\) \\((t,3t)\\)-woven. Its order is greater than \\(Kt\\ge t\\), so choose \\(t\\) roots and no pairs. Wovenness supplies a \\(K_t\\) model, contradicting \\(h(G)&lt;t\\).</p><p>For a nonempty graph, \\(h(G)\\ge1\\). Take\n\\[\nt=h(G)+1\\le2h(G)\n\\]\nin (55). Then\n\\[\n\\ell(G)\\le2(Q+6K)h(G).\n\\]\nThus \\(C=2(Q+6K)\\) is an absolute integer with all the asserted uniformity. The finite base range was covered in Proposition 11 before its induction began, so no small-parameter exception remains.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 1.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\chi(G)\\le Ch(G). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(G\\) is a finite nonempty simple graph.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\chi(G)\\) is its chromatic number.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(C\\) is the absolute integer supplied by Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\chi(G)\\le Ch(G).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A coloring from the common list\n\\[\n\\{1,\\ldots,\\ell(G)\\}\n\\]\nshows that \\(\\chi(G)\\le\\ell(G)\\). Theorem 1 therefore gives\n\\[\n\\chi(G)\\le\\ell(G)\\le Ch(G).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Theorems 2.1–2.3 and Lemmas 2.4–2.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The following probabilistic tools hold.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For a vertex set \\(X\\), \\(P[X]\\) denotes the induced subgraph, and \\(P-X=P[V(P)\\setminus X]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A graph is \\(k\\)-connected if it has more than \\(k\\) vertices and remains connected after deletion of fewer than \\(k\\) vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">A separator split is a partition \\(V(P)=A\\mathbin{\\dot\\cup}Z\\mathbin{\\dot\\cup}A'\\), where \\(A,A'\\) are nonempty and anticomplete.</p><p class=\"li\">All logarithms are natural. The null graph has \\(h=\\ell=\\chi=f=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The fractional chromatic number \\(f(P)\\) is the minimum total weight of nonnegative weights on the stable sets that cover every vertex with weight at least one. Finite linear-programming duality gives\n\\[\nf(P)=\n\\max\\left\\{\n\\sum_{v\\in V(P)}w(v):\nw(v)\\ge0,\\ \n\\sum_{v\\in I}w(v)\\le1\n\\text{ for every stable set }I\n\\right\\}. \\tag{1}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(r&gt;0\\), \\(f(P)\\le r\\) is equivalent to the existence of a distribution on stable sets in which every vertex is included with probability at least \\(1/r\\). Restriction to an induced subgraph cannot increase \\(f\\), and adding one vertex increases \\(f\\) by at most one.</p><p class=\"li\">The following are external structural inputs, not results proved in this paper:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. **Original Theorem 2.1, fractional minor bound:** \\(f(P)\\le4h(P)\\) for every graph \\(P\\). This follows from Reed and Seymour’s stronger bound \\(f(P)\\le2h(P)\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. **Original Theorem 2.2, ordinary-chromatic connectivity extraction:** if \\(k\\ge1\\) and \\(\\chi(P)\\ge7k\\), then \\(P\\) has a \\(k\\)-connected subgraph \\(J\\) with \\(\\chi(J)\\ge\\chi(P)-6k\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. **Original Theorem 2.3, grouping terminals:** there is an absolute integer \\(\\gamma\\ge1\\) such that, in a \\(\\gamma m\\)-connected graph, every partition of \\(m\\ge1\\) distinct terminals into nonempty groups can be realized by pairwise disjoint connected vertex sets, one containing each group.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following probabilistic tools hold.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(|V(P)|\\le n\\), \\(n\\ge1\\), and \\(f(P)\\le r\\), \\(r\\ge1\\), there is a distribution on stable sets \\(I\\) such that\n\\[\n|I|\\le\\lceil n/r\\rceil,\\qquad\n\\Pr(v\\in I)=\\frac1{2r}\n\\quad(v\\in V(P)).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(Z\\) is a function of \\(s\\ge1\\) independent finite random variables and changing one variable changes \\(Z\\) by at most \\(b&gt;0\\), then\n\\[\n\\Pr(|Z-\\mathbb EZ|\\ge u)\n\\le2\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{u^2}{2sb^2}\\right)\n\\quad(u&gt;0).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(P\\) is \\(k\\)-connected, has at most \\(n\\) vertices, and \\(k\\) is sufficiently large, put\n\\[\ns=\\left\\lceil10(n/k)\\log(2n)\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nThere is a family of at most \\((n+1)^{s+1}\\) matchings, each of size \\(\\lfloor k/8\\rfloor\\), such that the following implication holds: if \\(U\\subseteq V(P)\\), every vertex of \\(P\\) has at least \\(k/2\\) neighbors outside \\(U\\), and fewer than \\(k/32\\) endpoints of each matching belong to \\(U\\), then \\(P-U\\) is \\(\\lfloor k/32\\rfloor\\)-connected.</p>", "proof": "<p>The three asserted tools are Lemmas 1.1, 1.2, and 1.3.</p><p>For the fractional-coloring distribution equivalence used throughout, divide a fractional coloring of weight at most \\(r\\) by \\(r\\) and put any missing mass on the empty set. Conversely, multiply such a distribution by \\(r\\). The primal linear program is feasible using singleton stable sets and has a finite optimum, so the stated use of finite linear-programming duality has no feasibility or boundedness exception.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a distribution on stable sets \\(I\\) satisfying \\( |I|\\le\\lceil n/r\\rceil,\\qquad \\Pr(v\\in I)=\\frac1{2r} \\quad(v\\in V(P)). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\) is any graph, without a minor-exclusion assumption.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(|V(P)|\\le n\\), where \\(n\\ge1\\), and \\(f(P)\\le r\\), where \\(r\\ge1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a distribution on stable sets \\(I\\) satisfying\n\\[\n|I|\\le\\lceil n/r\\rceil,\\qquad\n\\Pr(v\\in I)=\\frac1{2r}\n\\quad(v\\in V(P)).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Start with a stable-set distribution having inclusion probabilities at least \\(1/r\\). Conditional independent deletion of each included vertex reduces every marginal to exactly \\(1/r\\) and preserves stability.</p><p>Put \\(M=\\lceil n/r\\rceil\\). Partition each resulting stable set \\(I\\) into parts of size at most \\(M\\), assigning its probability mass to each part. This gives a finite measure of total mass at most\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(1+|I|/M)\n=1+\\frac{|V(P)|}{rM}\\le2.\n\\]\nEvery vertex still has mass \\(1/r\\). Add mass on the empty set to make the total exactly two and divide by two.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(u>0\\), \\( \\Pr(|Z-\\mathbb EZ|\\ge u) \\le2\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{u^2}{2sb^2}\\right). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(Z\\) is a function of \\(s\\ge1\\) independent finite random variables.</p><p class=\"li\">Changing one variable changes \\(Z\\) by at most \\(b&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(u&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr(|Z-\\mathbb EZ|\\ge u)\n\\le2\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{u^2}{2sb^2}\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Expose the variables in order. The martingale of conditional expectations has differences of conditional mean zero and absolute value at most \\(b\\). Convexity bounds the conditional exponential moment of a difference at \\(\\lambda\\) by\n\\[\n\\cosh(\\lambda b)\\le\\exp(\\lambda^2b^2/2).\n\\]\nMultiplying these bounds and applying Markov’s inequality with \\(\\lambda=u/(sb^2)\\) gives the upper tail. Apply the same argument to \\(-Z\\) and add the two bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a family of at most \\((n+1)^{s+1}\\) matchings, each of size \\(\\lfloor k/8\\rfloor\\), such that, whenever \\(U\\subseteq V(P)\\), every vertex has at least \\(k/2\\) neighbors outside \\(U\\), and fewer than \\(k/32\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\) is a \\(k\\)-connected graph on at most \\(n\\) vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(k\\) is a sufficiently large integer.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n\\[\ns=\\left\\lceil10(n/k)\\log(2n)\\right\\rceil.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a family of at most \\((n+1)^{s+1}\\) matchings, each of size \\(\\lfloor k/8\\rfloor\\), such that, whenever \\(U\\subseteq V(P)\\), every vertex has at least \\(k/2\\) neighbors outside \\(U\\), and fewer than \\(k/32\\) endpoints of each matching belong to \\(U\\), the graph \\(P-U\\) is \\(\\lfloor k/32\\rfloor\\)-connected.</p>", "proof": "<p>For every set \\(R\\) of at most \\(s\\) vertices, put\n\\[\nB_R=\\bigcup_{v\\in R}N_P(v).\n\\]\nWhenever both \\(B_R\\) and its complement have size at least \\(k/3\\), fix a matching of size \\(\\lfloor k/8\\rfloor\\) across this cut. Such a matching exists: otherwise the endpoints of a maximal smaller matching form a vertex cover of the crossing edges of size less than \\(k/4\\). Both sides remain nonempty after their removal, contradicting \\(k\\)-connectivity. The number of sets \\(R\\), and hence of matchings, is at most \\((n+1)^{s+1}\\).</p><p>The neighborhood assumption ensures that \\(P-U\\) has more than \\(\\lfloor k/32\\rfloor\\) vertices. If it fails the asserted connectivity, there are\n\\[\nZ\\subseteq V(P)\\setminus U,\\qquad |Z|&lt;k/32,\n\\]\nand nonempty anticomplete sides \\(A,A'\\) partitioning \\(V(P)\\setminus(U\\cup Z)\\). Every vertex of either side has more than \\(k/3\\) neighbors in that same side. In particular both sides have size at least \\(k/3\\).</p><p>Make \\(s\\) independent uniform draws from \\(A\\). For each \\(v\\in A\\), the probability that none of the draws is an open neighbor of \\(v\\) is at most\n\\[\n\\exp(-ks/(3n)).\n\\]\nThe union bound over \\(A\\) is less than one. Thus the set \\(R\\) of drawn vertices, after repetitions are removed, satisfies \\(A\\subseteq B_R\\). Since \\(A,A'\\) are anticomplete, \\(A'\\cap B_R=\\varnothing\\).</p><p>The matching fixed for this cut has every edge incident with \\(U\\cup Z\\); otherwise an edge would join \\(A\\) to \\(A'\\). But \\(U\\) contains fewer than \\(k/32\\) of its endpoints, and \\(Z\\) contains fewer than \\(k/32\\) vertices. They cannot cover its \\(\\lfloor k/8\\rfloor\\) disjoint edges. This contradiction proves the lemma.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 3.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are absolute positive integers \\(L\\) and \\(t_0\\) such that, for every integer \\(t\\ge t_0\\), \\( h(G)<t,\\qquad |V(G)|\\le t^{11/10} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\ell(G)\\le Lt. \\) Auxiliary setting for the lemmas below:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(G\\) is a finite graph.</p><p class=\"li\">The constants \\(\\gamma\\) and the external structural inputs are those in Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are absolute positive integers \\(L\\) and \\(t_0\\) such that, for every integer \\(t\\ge t_0\\),\n\\[\nh(G)&lt;t,\\qquad |V(G)|\\le t^{11/10}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\ell(G)\\le Lt.\n\\]</p><p>Auxiliary setting for the lemmas below:</p><p class=\"li\">Fix\n\\[\nD_0=10^6\\gamma,\\qquad Q_0=401^2, \\tag{2}\n\\]\nand choose a positive integer \\(B\\) such that\n\\[\nB\\ge16,\\qquad\n2^{B/4}\\ge\n\\max\\{2000(D_0+1),10^4Q_0^2\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For sufficiently large \\(t\\), let\n\\[\n1\\le n\\le t^{11/10},\\qquad\n\\frac{t}{\\log t}\\le r\\le4t, \\tag{4}\n\\]\nand let \\(P\\) satisfy \\(|V(P)|\\le n\\), \\(h(P)&lt;t\\), and \\(f(P)\\le r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\ny=\\max\\{1,t/r\\},\\quad\nx=\\lfloor r/200\\rfloor,\\quad\nq=\\lceil t/x\\rceil^2,\\quad\nk=\\lceil D_0ty\\rceil,\n\\]\n\\[\nN=\\lceil Br\\log(2y)\\rceil,\\qquad\nM=\\lceil n/r\\rceil. \\tag{5}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix eligibility sets \\(E_1,\\ldots,E_N\\subseteq V(P)\\), each vertex belonging to at least \\(N/2\\) of them.</p><p class=\"li\">Independently sample stable sets \\(I_j\\) using Proposition 1, with \\(|I_j|\\le M\\) and \\(\\Pr(v\\in I_j)=1/(2r)\\), and set\n\\[\nW=V(P)\\setminus\\bigcup_{j=1}^N(I_j\\cap E_j). \\tag{8}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Every vertex satisfies\n\\[\n\\Pr(v\\in W)\\le\np:=\\exp(-N/(4r))\\le(2y)^{-B/4}. \\tag{9}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">All cutoffs below are uniform over these data and the eligibility sets.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix sufficiently large \\(t\\), and let \\(G\\) satisfy the proposition. The empty graph needs no colors, so assume \\(G\\) is nonempty.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nr_j=4t2^{-j},\\qquad y_j=\\max\\{1,t/r_j\\},\n\\]\nand let \\(J\\) be the largest nonnegative integer with \\(r_J\\ge t/\\log t\\). For each \\(j=0,\\ldots,J\\), allocate a separate block of\n\\[\nN_j=\\lceil Br_j\\log(2y_j)\\rceil\n\\]\nbins, and allocate \\(16t\\) further cleanup bins. All bins are distinct. Since \\(y_j\\le2^j\\),\n\\[\nn_{\\mathrm{bin}}\n\\le4Bt\\log2\\sum_{j\\ge0}(j+1)2^{-j}\n+(J+1)+16t\n=16B(\\log2)t+(J+1)+16t.\n\\]\nAlso \\(J+1=O(\\log\\log t)\\). Choose an integer\n\\[\nD_{\\mathrm{bin}}\\ge16B\\log2+18.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(t\\), \\(n_{\\mathrm{bin}}\\le D_{\\mathrm{bin}}t\\). Choose a positive integer \\(L\\) with\n\\[\n\\frac{L}{2D_{\\mathrm{bin}}}&gt;\\log2+1. \\tag{14}\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a finite list assignment with \\(|\\mathcal L(v)|\\ge Lt\\). Map every color in its finite union independently and uniformly to one of the \\(n_{\\mathrm{bin}}\\) bins.</p><p>For a fixed vertex and a block of \\(u\\) bins, failure to have an allowable color in at least half the bins implies that some \\(\\lceil u/2\\rceil\\) bins are missed. For any specified such subset, the probability is at most\n\\[\n\\left(1-\\frac{u}{2n_{\\mathrm{bin}}}\\right)^{|\\mathcal L(v)|}\n\\le\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{Ltu}{2n_{\\mathrm{bin}}}\\right)\n\\le\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{Lu}{2D_{\\mathrm{bin}}}\\right).\n\\]\nThere are at most \\(2^u\\) subsets, so the failure probability is at most \\(e^{-u}\\).</p><p>Every stage block has\n\\[\nu\\ge B(\\log2)t/\\log t,\n\\]\nand the cleanup block has \\(u=16t\\). With \\(c_B=\\min\\{B\\log2,16\\}&gt;0\\), the union bound over vertices and blocks is at most\n\\[\n(J+2)t^{11/10}\\exp(-c_Bt/\\log t)=o(1).\n\\]\nFix a map for which every vertex has an allowable color in at least half of every block. This estimate is independent of the number of distinct colors in the palette.</p><p>A vertex is eligible for a bin if its list contains a color mapped to that bin. The eligibility remains fixed when vertices survive to later stages. Initially, the fractional minor bound gives \\(f(G)\\le4t=r_0\\).</p><p>At stage \\(j\\), let \\(P\\) be the current induced remainder. If nonempty, it satisfies\n\\[\nh(P)&lt;t,\\qquad |V(P)|\\le t^{11/10},\\qquad f(P)\\le r_j.\n\\]\nApply Lemma 2.5 to the eligibility sets of that stage. Retain one covering bin for every removed vertex. The remainder has fractional chromatic number at most \\(r_j/2=r_{j+1}\\).</p><p>After stage \\(J\\), the remainder has fractional chromatic number less than\n\\[\n\\rho=t/\\log t.\n\\]\nIf nonempty, independently sample a stable set in each cleanup bin from a fractional coloring distribution with inclusion probability at least \\(1/\\rho\\). Each remaining vertex is eligible in at least \\(8t\\) cleanup bins, and is missed by all eligible samples with probability at most\n\\[\n(1-1/\\rho)^{8t}\\le e^{-8t/\\rho}=t^{-8}.\n\\]\nThe probability any vertex is missed is at most \\(t^{11/10}t^{-8}=t^{-69/10}&lt;1\\). Fix a covering outcome and retain one covering bin for each remaining vertex.</p><p>Every vertex now belongs to the stable set of its retained bin. Choose any color in its original list mapped to that bin. Vertices receiving the same color have the same retained bin and lie in its stable set, so are nonadjacent.</p><p>The constants were chosen in the order \\(\\gamma,B,D_{\\mathrm{bin}},L\\). All cutoffs depend only on these constants, and the thinning estimates are uniform over intermediate graphs and eligibility sets. Enlarging one absolute \\(t_0\\) proves the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For each fixed \\(\\delta>0\\), and all sufficiently large \\(t\\), \\( \\Pr(|Z-\\mathbb EZ|\\ge\\delta r) \\le2\\exp(-t^{7/10}). \\) The cutoff depends only on \\(\\delta\\) and the fixed constants.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the auxiliary setting of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Take at most \\(q\\) independent copies \\(W_i\\) of the experiment defining \\(W\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(A\\subseteq V(P)\\) is fixed before sampling.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Z\\) is one of\n\\[\n|A\\cap W_i|,\\qquad\n\\left|A\\cap\\bigcup_iW_i\\right|,\\qquad\nf(P[A\\cap W_i]).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each fixed \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), and all sufficiently large \\(t\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr(|Z-\\mathbb EZ|\\ge\\delta r)\n\\le2\\exp(-t^{7/10}). \\tag{11}\n\\]\nThe cutoff depends only on \\(\\delta\\) and the fixed constants.</p>", "proof": "<p>Changing one sampled stable set changes \\(W_i\\) on at most \\(2M\\) vertices. It changes a union of remainders on at most the same number of vertices. Adding or deleting a vertex changes fractional chromatic number by at most one, so all the stated quantities have bounded differences at most \\(2M\\) per trial.</p><p>The auxiliary parameters satisfy\n\\[\n1\\le y\\le\\log t,\\qquad\n\\frac{t}{y}\\le r\\le\\frac{4t}{y},\\qquad\nx\\ge r/400,\n\\]\n\\[\nq\\le Q_0y^2,\\qquad\nxq\\le403ty,\\qquad\nD_0ty\\le k\\le(D_0+1)ty. \\tag{6}\n\\]\nIndeed,\n\\[\nt/x\\le400t/r\\le400y,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nx\\lceil t/x\\rceil^2\n\\le t^2/x+2t+x\n\\le400t^2/r+2t+r/200\n\\le403ty.\n\\]</p><p>For sufficiently large \\(t\\), \\(r\\log(2y)\\ge1\\), so\n\\[\nN\\le(B+1)r\\log(2y).\n\\]\nWith \\(A_0=4Q_0(B+1)\\), this gives\n\\[\nqN\\le A_0ty\\log(2y),\\qquad\nM\\le2t^{1/10}y.\n\\]\nThe bounded-differences estimate in Proposition 1 yields\n\\[\n\\Pr(|Z-\\mathbb EZ|\\ge\\delta r)\n\\le\n2\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\delta^2r^2}\n{2(qN)(2M)^2}\\right)\n\\le\n2\\exp\\!\\left(\n-\\frac{\\delta^2t^{4/5}}\n{32A_0y^5\\log(2y)}\n\\right).\n\\]\nSince \\(1\\le y\\le\\log t\\), the final exponent has magnitude at least \\(t^{7/10}\\) for all sufficiently large \\(t\\). Independence between different vertices is not used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a fixed induced \\(k\\)-connected subgraph \\(H\\subseteq P\\) such that \\( \\mathbb E f(H[W\\cap V(H)])>r/4. \\) The graph \\(H\\) is chosen before any fresh copies of the experiment are sampled.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the auxiliary setting and experiment of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Every outcome of positive probability satisfies \\(f(P[W])&gt;r/2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a fixed induced \\(k\\)-connected subgraph \\(H\\subseteq P\\) such that\n\\[\n\\mathbb E f(H[W\\cap V(H)])&gt;r/4. \\tag{12}\n\\]\nThe graph \\(H\\) is chosen before any fresh copies of the experiment are sampled.</p>", "proof": "<p>Build a binary decomposition of \\(P\\), making all choices before sampling. A node is an induced subgraph. Stop at a node of order at most \\(2k\\), and also at a larger node that is \\(k\\)-connected. Otherwise choose a separator of size less than \\(k\\), partition the remaining vertices into two nonempty anticomplete sides, and form two children by adding the separator to each side.</p><p>There are \\(O(n^2+1)\\) nodes. Give a node of order \\(u&gt;2k\\) potential \\(u-k\\). Each large child’s potential is strictly smaller than its parent’s. If both children are large, their potentials sum to at most their parent’s because their orders sum to \\(u+|S|&lt;u+k\\). If just one child is large, its potential alone is at most the parent’s. At each depth the sum of large-node potentials is at most \\(n\\), and a large-node branch has depth at most \\(n\\). The small leaves contribute at most two children per large node, besides a possible small root.</p><p>The choice of \\(B\\) gives\n\\[\n2kp\\le r/1000,\\qquad q^2p\\le10^{-4}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\nFor the first inequality,\n\\[\n\\frac{2kp}{r}\n\\le2(D_0+1)y^2(2y)^{-B/4}\n\\le2(D_0+1)2^{-B/4}.\n\\]\nFor the second,\n\\[\nq^2p\\le Q_0^2y^4(2y)^{-B/4}\n\\le Q_0^22^{-B/4}.\n\\]</p><p>Every separator and every small leaf has order at most \\(2k\\), so its expected intersection with \\(W\\) has size at most \\(r/1000\\). Lemma 2.1 and a union bound show that, with probability \\(1-o(1)\\), all these intersections have fewer than \\(r/100\\) vertices.</p><p>Suppose every \\(k\\)-connected leaf had expected fractional chromatic number at most \\(r/4\\) after restriction to \\(W\\). Lemma 2.1 then shows, with probability \\(1-o(1)\\), that every such leaf restriction has fractional chromatic number less than \\(3r/10\\). A small leaf has this bound as well, since fractional chromatic number is at most order.</p><p>Fix an outcome satisfying these conclusions and write \\(F=P[W]\\). The hypothesis gives \\(f(F)&gt;r/2\\), but every leaf restriction has fractional chromatic number less than \\(3r/10\\).</p><p>For a positive integer \\(m\\), replace each vertex of \\(F\\) by a clique of order \\(m\\), with complete joins corresponding to edges, obtaining \\(F^{(m)}\\). Every color class projects to a stable set of \\(F\\) and uses at most one vertex of each replacement clique. Dividing the multiplicities of projected stable sets by \\(m\\) gives a fractional coloring of \\(F\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\chi(F^{(m)})\\ge mf(F)&gt;mr/2. \\tag{13}\n\\]</p><p>For each leaf restriction, choose a fractional coloring of weight less than \\(3r/10\\), and round its finitely many stable-set weights up to multiples of \\(1/m\\). Give a distinct color slot to each unit of weight \\(1/m\\). Each original vertex belongs to at least \\(m\\) slots, so its copies can be assigned distinct slots containing it. Each slot is stable upstairs. Rounding adds only a bounded number of slots, independent of \\(m\\) for the fixed leaf. Thus, for all sufficiently large \\(m\\), every blown-up leaf restriction is colorable with fewer than \\(31mr/100\\) colors.</p><p>Apply the external ordinary-chromatic connectivity extraction in Proposition 1 to \\(F^{(m)}\\), with\n\\[\nk'=\\lceil mr/50\\rceil.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(m\\), \\(\\chi(F^{(m)})&gt;7k'\\), and the resulting \\(k'\\)-connected subgraph \\(J\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\chi(J)\\ge\\chi(F^{(m)})-6k'\n&gt;19mr/50-6&gt;31mr/100.\n\\]\nEach restricted separator has fewer than \\(r/100\\) vertices, so its blow-up has fewer than \\(mr/100&lt;k'\\) vertices. Thus \\(J\\) cannot meet both exclusive sides of a split, and cannot be contained in its separator because \\(|V(J)|&gt;k'\\). Descending the decomposition places \\(J\\) inside one blown-up leaf restriction, contradicting its coloring bound.</p><p>Therefore some \\(k\\)-connected leaf satisfies the required expectation. The argument also covers the case where all leaves are small. The blow-up is only an auxiliary graph for an extraction theorem requiring no minor exclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "With failure probability at most \\(4q(q-1)p+o(1)\\), every \\(H[T_i]\\) has fractional chromatic number greater than \\(r/8\\), and hence contains a \\(K_{2x}\\) model.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the auxiliary setting of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Every positive-probability outcome has \\(f(P[W])&gt;r/2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(H\\) supplied by Lemma 2.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Sample \\(q\\) fresh independent copies \\(W_1,\\ldots,W_q\\), and define\n\\[\nT_i=V(H)\\cap\\left(W_i\\setminus\\bigcup_{j\\ne i}W_j\\right),\n\\qquad\nU=V(H)\\cap\\bigcup_{i=1}^qW_i.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With failure probability at most \\(4q(q-1)p+o(1)\\), every \\(H[T_i]\\) has fractional chromatic number greater than \\(r/8\\), and hence contains a \\(K_{2x}\\) model. These models are vertex-disjoint.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemmas 2.2 and 2.1, the events\n\\[\nA_i=\\{f(H[W_i\\cap V(H)])&gt;r/5\\}\n\\]\nall hold with probability \\(1-o(1)\\): each failure probability is at most \\(2\\exp(-t^{7/10})\\), and \\(q\\le Q_0(\\log t)^2\\).</p><p>For each outcome of \\(W_i\\) satisfying \\(A_i\\), choose optimal dual weights on \\(H[W_i\\cap V(H)]\\), of total weight \\(a_i&gt;r/5\\), as a function of \\(W_i\\) alone. Conditional on this one copy, each other copy still satisfies the vertex survival bound \\(\\Pr(v\\in W_j)\\le p\\). The expected weight on vertices belonging to another copy is at most \\((q-1)pa_i\\). Markov’s inequality shows that the probability of \\(A_i\\) together with loss greater than \\(a_i/4\\) is at most \\(4(q-1)p\\).</p><p>A union bound over \\(i\\) shows that, except with probability \\(4q(q-1)p+o(1)\\), all private sets retain weight at least\n\\[\n3a_i/4&gt;3r/20&gt;r/8.\n\\]\nRestriction to \\(T_i\\) preserves dual feasibility, so \\(f(H[T_i])&gt;r/8\\). Only one copy at a time has been conditioned on, not the simultaneous events \\(A_1,\\ldots,A_q\\).</p><p>The fractional minor bound in Proposition 1 gives\n\\[\nh(H[T_i])&gt;r/32&gt;2\\lfloor r/200\\rfloor=2x.\n\\]\nThe sets \\(T_i\\) are pairwise disjoint, so the models are vertex-disjoint.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Lemma 3.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "With probability \\(1-o(1)\\), every vertex of \\(H\\) has at least \\(k/2\\) neighbors in \\(H-U\\), and \\(H-U\\) is \\(\\lfloor k/32\\rfloor\\)-connected.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the same \\(P,t,n,r,x,q,k,p,H,W_i,U\\) as in Lemma 2.3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With probability \\(1-o(1)\\), every vertex of \\(H\\) has at least \\(k/2\\) neighbors in \\(H-U\\), and \\(H-U\\) is \\(\\lfloor k/32\\rfloor\\)-connected.</p>", "proof": "<p>Each fixed vertex belongs to \\(U\\) with probability at most \\(qp\\le10^{-4}\\). Before sampling, fix \\(k\\) neighbors of each vertex of \\(H\\). The expected number of these neighbors in \\(U\\) is at most \\(k/10000\\). A count of at least \\(k/2\\) requires a deviation greater than \\(k/3\\ge r/3\\). Lemma 2.1 and a union bound over at most \\(n\\) vertices prove the neighborhood assertion with probability \\(1-o(1)\\), including for vertices in \\(U\\).</p><p>For connectivity, fix before sampling the cut-test family from Proposition 1 for \\(H\\), with\n\\[\ns=\\left\\lceil10(n/k)\\log(2n)\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nThe number of tests is at most\n\\[\n(n+1)^{s+1}\n=\\exp(O((1+n/k)\\log^2(2n)))\n\\le\\exp(O(t^{1/10}\\log^2t)).\n\\]\nThe endpoint set of a matching has size at most \\(k/4\\), and expected intersection with \\(U\\) at most \\(k/40000\\). A count of at least \\(k/32\\) requires a deviation greater than \\(k/64\\ge r/64\\). Lemma 2.1 and the bound on the number of tests show that, with probability \\(1-o(1)\\), every matching has fewer than \\(k/32\\) endpoints in \\(U\\). The cut test now gives the asserted connectivity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.5", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, eligible thinning", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are stable sets \\(I_1,\\ldots,I_N\\), each of size at most \\(\\lceil n/r\\rceil\\), such that \\( f\\left(P-\\bigcup_{j=1}^N(I_j\\cap E_j)\\right)\\le r/2. \\) The cutoff is uniform over all the stated data.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(t\\) is sufficiently large.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(1\\le n\\le t^{11/10}\\) and \\(t/\\log t\\le r\\le4t\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(|V(P)|\\le n\\), \\(h(P)&lt;t\\), and \\(f(P)\\le r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(B\\) is the constant fixed in Proposition 2, and\n\\[\nN=\\left\\lceil Br\\log\\bigl(2\\max\\{1,t/r\\}\\bigr)\\right\\rceil.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(E_1,\\ldots,E_N\\subseteq V(P)\\) contain every vertex at least \\(N/2\\) times.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are stable sets \\(I_1,\\ldots,I_N\\), each of size at most \\(\\lceil n/r\\rceil\\), such that\n\\[\nf\\left(P-\\bigcup_{j=1}^N(I_j\\cap E_j)\\right)\\le r/2.\n\\]\nThe cutoff is uniform over all the stated data.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(P\\) is empty, take all stable sets empty. Otherwise use the sampling experiment of Proposition 2, and suppose every positive-probability outcome has \\(f(P[W])&gt;r/2\\).</p><p>Lemma 2.2 supplies a fixed \\(H\\). The parameter estimates and the choice of \\(D_0\\) give, for sufficiently large \\(t\\),\n\\[\nk\\ge r,\\qquad\n2xq&lt;k/64,\\qquad\n\\lfloor k/32\\rfloor\\ge\\gamma(2xq). \\tag{7}\n\\]\nBy \\(q^2p\\le10^{-4}\\), the failure probability in Lemma 2.3 is at most \\(4\\cdot10^{-4}+o(1)\\). Thus Lemmas 2.3 and 2.4 hold simultaneously with positive probability. Fix such an outcome, including its \\(q\\) disjoint \\(K_{2x}\\) models.</p><p>Partition \\(t\\) labels into\n\\[\nb=\\lceil t/x\\rceil\n\\]\nnonempty blocks of size at most \\(x\\). Assign a different private model to every single block and every unordered pair of distinct blocks. There are\n\\[\nb+\\binom b2=b(b+1)/2\\le b^2=q\n\\]\nassignments. In a model assigned to one block, retain one bag for each label in that block. In a model assigned to two blocks, retain one bag for each label in their union. At most \\(2x\\) bags are retained per model, and every pair of labels occurs together in one assigned model.</p><p>Choose a port in every retained bag. There are at most \\(2xq\\) ports, each with at least \\(k/2\\) neighbors in \\(H-U\\). Choose distinct such neighbors greedily. Their actual number \\(m\\) satisfies \\(1\\le m\\le2xq\\), and their labels partition them into \\(t\\) nonempty groups. Since\n\\[\n\\operatorname{conn}(H-U)\\ge\\lfloor k/32\\rfloor\n\\ge\\gamma(2xq)\\ge\\gamma m,\n\\]\nthe terminal grouping input in Proposition 1 gives disjoint connected sets in \\(H-U\\), one for each label.</p><p>For each label, unite its connected set with all its assigned private bags and port edges. These sets are connected and disjoint. Every two are adjacent because their labels occur together in an assigned clique model. They form a \\(K_t\\) model in \\(P\\), contrary to \\(h(P)&lt;t\\). Hence some sampling outcome has fractional chromatic number at most \\(r/2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemmas 4.1 and 4.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\(H\\) is a subgraph of \\(P\\), then \\(\\ell(H)\\le\\ell(P)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\) is a finite graph.</p><p class=\"li\">A graph is \\(q\\)-degenerate if every nonempty subgraph has a vertex of degree at most \\(q\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(H\\) is a subgraph of \\(P\\), then \\(\\ell(H)\\le\\ell(P)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For every \\(Z\\subseteq V(P)\\),\n\\[\n\\ell(P-Z)\\ge\\ell(P)-|Z|.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(P\\) is \\(q\\)-degenerate, \\(q\\ge0\\), then \\(\\ell(P)\\le q+1\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. For every integer \\(k\\ge1\\), if \\(\\ell(P)&gt;6k\\), then some induced \\(k\\)-connected subgraph \\(H\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\ell(H)\\ge\\ell(P)-6k.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The first three assertions are Lemma 3.1.</p><p>For connectivity extraction, suppose \\(\\ell(P)&gt;6k\\) but no induced \\(k\\)-connected \\(H\\) has \\(\\ell(H)\\ge\\ell(P)-6k\\). Put\n\\[\nm=\\ell(P)-6k-1\\ge0.\n\\]\nEvery induced \\(k\\)-connected subgraph has list chromatic number at most \\(m\\). Apply Lemma 3.2 with no precolored vertices and no forbidden colors. Every list assignment of size at least\n\\[\nm+6k=\\ell(P)-1\n\\]\nis colorable, contradicting the definition of \\(\\ell(P)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\ell(H)\\le\\ell(P),\\qquad \\ell(P-Z)\\ge\\ell(P)-|Z|, \\) and, if \\(P\\) is \\(q\\)-degenerate, then \\(\\ell(P)\\le q+1\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\) is a finite graph.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(H\\) is a subgraph of \\(P\\), \\(Z\\subseteq V(P)\\), and \\(q\\ge0\\) is an integer.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\ell(H)\\le\\ell(P),\\qquad\n\\ell(P-Z)\\ge\\ell(P)-|Z|,\n\\]\nand, if \\(P\\) is \\(q\\)-degenerate, then \\(\\ell(P)\\le q+1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For monotonicity, extend any list assignment on \\(H\\) with lists of size at least \\(\\ell(P)\\) to the other vertices of \\(P\\), retaining the lists on \\(V(H)\\). A list coloring of \\(P\\) restricts to one of \\(H\\); additional edges among \\(V(H)\\) cause no difficulty.</p><p>For deletion, put \\(s=\\ell(P-Z)\\) and give every vertex a list of size at least \\(s+|Z|\\). Greedily color \\(P[Z]\\), avoiding at most \\(|Z|-1\\) colors at any step. Delete from each remaining list the colors used on its neighbors in \\(Z\\). At most \\(|Z|\\) entries are removed, so \\(P-Z\\) can be colored. Thus \\(\\ell(P)\\le s+|Z|\\), including the empty-graph cases.</p><p>For degeneracy, repeatedly delete a vertex of degree at most \\(q\\). In reverse deletion order every vertex has at most \\(q\\) already colored neighbors, so lists of size \\(q+1\\) suffice.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(P\\) has a proper \\(\\mathcal L\\)-coloring extending \\(\\varphi\\) and avoiding \\(B_v\\) at each \\(v\\notin A\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(k\\ge1\\) and \\(m\\ge0\\) are integers.</p><p class=\"li\">Every induced \\(k\\)-connected subgraph of \\(P\\) has list chromatic number at most \\(m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Every vertex has a list \\(\\mathcal L(v)\\) of size at least \\(m+6k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A set \\(A\\subseteq V(P)\\) has a proper list precoloring \\(\\varphi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(v\\notin A\\), a finite forbidden-color set \\(B_v\\) satisfies \\(|B_v|&lt;k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The constraints satisfy\n\\[\nk|A|+\\sum_{v\\notin A}|B_v|\\le2k^2. \\tag{15}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(P\\) has a proper \\(\\mathcal L\\)-coloring extending \\(\\varphi\\) and avoiding \\(B_v\\) at each \\(v\\notin A\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct on \\(|V(P)|\\). The induced-subgraph hypothesis is inherited, and the budget gives \\(|A|\\le2k\\).</p><p>If \\(|V(P)|\\le2k\\), extend \\(\\varphi\\) greedily. At each step at most \\(2k-1\\) colors are used by colored vertices and at most \\(k-1\\) are forbidden.</p><p>If \\(P\\) is \\(k\\)-connected, then \\(\\ell(P)\\le m\\). At each \\(v\\notin A\\), remove \\(B_v\\) and the colors on its precolored neighbors. The remaining list has size at least\n\\[\nm+6k-|A|-|B_v|\\ge m+3k+1\\ge m.\n\\]\nLemma 3.1 gives \\(\\ell(P-A)\\le m\\), so these lists color \\(P-A\\). Combine with \\(\\varphi\\).</p><p>Otherwise \\(|V(P)|&gt;2k\\), and there is a separator split\n\\[\nV(P)=V_1\\mathbin{\\dot\\cup}Z\\mathbin{\\dot\\cup}V_2,\n\\qquad |Z|&lt;k.\n\\]\nAssign costs\n\\[\nw(v)=\n\\begin{cases}\nk,&amp;v\\in A,\\\\\n|B_v|,&amp;v\\notin A,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nw(U)=\\sum_{v\\in U}w(v).\n\\]\nInterchange the sides so that \\(w(V_1)\\ge w(V_2)\\). Then\n\\[\nw(V_2)\\le k^2. \\tag{16}\n\\]</p><p>First color \\(P_1=P[V_1\\cup Z]\\). Retain the precoloring on \\(A_1=A\\cap(V_1\\cup Z)\\). At the other vertices use\n\\[\nB'_v=\n\\begin{cases}\nB_v\\cup\\{\\varphi(x):x\\in A\\cap V_2,\\ xv\\in E(P)\\},\n&amp;v\\in Z\\setminus A,\\\\\nB_v,&amp;v\\in V_1\\setminus A.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nTheir total cost satisfies\n\\[\nk|A_1|+\\sum_{v\\in V(P_1)\\setminus A_1}|B'_v|\n\\le w(V(P))-w(V_2)+|Z|\\,|A\\cap V_2|\n\\]\n\\[\n\\le w(V(P))-k|A\\cap V_2|+(k-1)|A\\cap V_2|\n\\le2k^2. \\tag{17}\n\\]\nRepeated colors can only reduce the left side.</p><p>Every \\(B'_v\\) has size at most\n\\[\n(k-1)+|A\\cap V_2|\\le3k-1.\n\\]\nConvert each vertex with \\(|B'_v|\\ge k\\) into a precolored vertex, one at a time. Choose a color avoiding \\(B'_v\\) and its currently precolored neighbors, then discard its forbidden set. Its cost changes from \\(|B'_v|\\) to \\(k\\), so the budget does not increase and at most \\(2k\\) vertices are precolored. Each choice avoids at most\n\\[\n(3k-1)+2k=5k-1&lt;m+6k\n\\]\ncolors. Every promoted vertex lies in \\(Z\\setminus A\\), and its color avoids the precolored neighbors in \\(V_2\\).</p><p>The resulting constraints on \\(P_1\\) satisfy the lemma. Since \\(V_2\\ne\\varnothing\\), induction supplies a coloring \\(\\psi_1\\). It respects the original constraints and is compatible across the separator with all precolored vertices in \\(V_2\\).</p><p>Next color \\(P_2=P[V_2\\cup Z]\\). Precolor \\(Z\\) according to \\(\\psi_1\\), retain \\(\\varphi\\) on \\(A\\cap V_2\\), and retain \\(B_v\\) for \\(v\\in V_2\\setminus A\\). The precoloring is proper, and its constraint cost is\n\\[\nk|Z|+w(V_2)\\le k(k-1)+k^2&lt;2k^2.\n\\]\nThe remaining forbidden sets have size less than \\(k\\), and \\(V_1\\ne\\varnothing\\), so induction applies. The two colorings agree on \\(Z\\) and combine because \\(V_1,V_2\\) are anticomplete.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 4.5, additive list-coloring loss", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\ell(P-X-Y)\\ge\\ell(P)-c_dt. \\) The cutoff is independent of \\(P\\), its order, and all list assignments and their palettes.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(L\\) is the integer in Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">For each fixed integer \\(d\\ge0\\), put\n\\[\nc_d=4L+d+5.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(t\\) is sufficiently large, \\(h(P)&lt;t\\), and \\(X,Y\\subseteq V(P)\\) are disjoint.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(|X|\\le t^{11/10}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(P[Y]\\) is \\(dt\\)-degenerate.</p><p class=\"li\">Every vertex outside \\(X\\cup Y\\) has at most \\(dt\\) neighbors in \\(Y\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\ell(P-X-Y)\\ge\\ell(P)-c_dt.\n\\]\nThe cutoff is independent of \\(P\\), its order, and all list assignments and their palettes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(R=P-X-Y\\), \\(s=\\ell(R)\\), and\n\\[\nu=s+(4L+d+5)t.\n\\]\nGiven lists of size at least \\(u\\), restrict every list to exactly \\(u\\) entries. Denote the assignment by \\(\\mathcal L\\), with finite palette\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=\\bigcup_{v\\in V(P)}\\mathcal L(v).\n\\]</p><p>Independently reserve each color of \\(\\mathcal C\\) with probability\n\\[\np=\\frac{2Lt}{u}.\n\\]\nReservation is global: every occurrence of a color is reserved together. At any vertex the number \\(Z_v\\) of reserved list entries has distribution \\(\\operatorname{Bin}(u,p)\\), with mean \\(\\mu=2Lt\\), independent of \\(s\\). Overlaps between different lists do not affect this marginal distribution.</p><p>For every real \\(\\lambda\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb Ee^{\\lambda Z_v}\n=(1-p+pe^\\lambda)^u\n\\le\\exp(\\mu(e^\\lambda-1)).\n\\]\nMarkov’s inequality with \\(\\lambda=-\\log2\\) and \\(\\lambda=\\log2\\) gives\n\\[\n\\Pr(Z_v&lt;\\mu/2)\n\\le\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{1-\\log2}{2}\\mu\\right), \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Pr(Z_v&gt;2\\mu)\n\\le\\exp(-(2\\log2-1)\\mu). \\tag{19}\n\\]</p><p>Let\n\\[\nT=\\{v\\in V(P)\\setminus X:|N_P(v)\\cap X|\\ge t\\}.\n\\]\nLemma 4.1 gives \\(|T|\\le\\binom{|X|}{2}\\). With\n\\[\n\\eta=\\min\\{(1-\\log2)/2,\\ 2\\log2-1\\}&gt;0,\n\\]\nthe probability that \\(Z_x&lt;Lt\\) for some \\(x\\in X\\), or \\(Z_v&gt;4Lt\\) for some \\(v\\in T\\), is at most\n\\[\n(|X|+|T|)e^{-2\\eta Lt}\n\\le2t^{11/5}e^{-2\\eta Lt}&lt;1\n\\]\nfor sufficiently large \\(t\\). Only polynomially many vertices are tested, independently of ambient order. Fix a successful reservation.</p><p>Color \\(P[X]\\) from reserved entries by Proposition 2, obtaining \\(\\varphi_X\\). For \\(v\\notin X\\), put\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1(v)=\n\\mathcal L(v)\\setminus\n\\{\\varphi_X(x):x\\in N_P(v)\\cap X\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(v\\in T\\), at most \\(4Lt\\) entries are deleted, since every deleted color is reserved. If \\(v\\notin T\\), fewer than \\(t\\) entries are deleted. Thus\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_1(v)|\n\\ge u-(4L+1)t\n=s+(d+4)t. \\tag{20}\n\\]</p><p>This bound is at least \\(dt+1\\), so Proposition 3 colors the \\(dt\\)-degenerate graph \\(P[Y]\\) from these lists, obtaining \\(\\varphi_Y\\). It has no conflict with \\(\\varphi_X\\).</p><p>Finally delete from each remaining list the colors used on neighbors in \\(Y\\). At most \\(dt\\) entries are deleted, leaving at least\n\\[\nu-(4L+1)t-dt=s+4t\\ge s.\n\\]\nThe definition of \\(s\\) colors \\(R\\). These colorings combine properly. Therefore\n\\[\n\\ell(P)\\le u=\\ell(P-X-Y)+c_dt.\n\\]\nOnly the cutoff in Proposition 2 and the exponential-versus-polynomial estimate require sufficiently large \\(t\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\left|\\{v\\in V(P)\\setminus X:|N_P(v)\\cap X|\\ge t\\}\\right| \\le\\binom{|X|}{2}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(t\\ge2\\) is an integer.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(h(P)&lt;t\\), and \\(X\\subseteq V(P)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\left|\\{v\\in V(P)\\setminus X:|N_P(v)\\cap X|\\ge t\\}\\right|\n\\le\\binom{|X|}{2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Process the indicated outside vertices in any order. Assign each an unused unordered pair of its neighbors in \\(X\\).</p><p>If this fails at \\(v\\), choose \\(t\\) distinct vertices in \\(N_P(v)\\cap X\\). Every pair among them has already been assigned to a different previously processed outside vertex adjacent to both. The corresponding length-two paths have distinct internal vertices outside \\(X\\), and form a subdivision of \\(K_t\\). This gives a \\(K_t\\) minor, a contradiction. The assignment is therefore an injection into the unordered pairs from \\(X\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemmas 5.1–5.4 and Corollary 5.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an absolute constant \\(C_*>0\\), for example \\(C_*=10^4\\), such that, for every integer \\(t\\ge2\\), every nonempty \\(P\\) with \\(h(P)<t\\) satisfies \\( d(P)\\le C_*t\\log(2t). \\) Every graph with \\(h(P)<t\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For a nonempty graph \\(R\\), write \\(e(R)=|E(R)|\\) and\n\\[\nd(R)=e(R)/|V(R)|.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\epsilon=1/200\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Graph-order bounds by real powers are ordinary real inequalities.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. There is an absolute constant \\(C_*&gt;0\\), for example \\(C_*=10^4\\), such that, for every integer \\(t\\ge2\\), every nonempty \\(P\\) with \\(h(P)&lt;t\\) satisfies\n\\[\nd(P)\\le C_*t\\log(2t).\n\\]\nEvery graph with \\(h(P)&lt;t\\) consequently satisfies\n\\[\n\\ell(P)\\le2C_*t\\log(2t)+1.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. There is an absolute positive integer \\(B_*\\) such that, for all sufficiently large \\(t\\), if \\(h(P)&lt;t\\) and \\(A,T\\subseteq V(P)\\) are disjoint, then\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{v\\in T\\\\|N_P(v)\\cap A|\\ge B_*t}}\n|N_P(v)\\cap A|\n\\le t^{1+2\\epsilon}|A|, \\tag{21}\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n|\\{v\\in T:|N_P(v)\\cap A|\\ge B_*t\\}|\n\\le t^{2\\epsilon}|A|.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For every fixed positive integer \\(K\\), there is an integer\n\\[\nD_K&gt;\\max(400B_*,12800K)\n\\]\nsuch that, for all sufficiently large \\(t\\), every nonempty \\(P\\) with\n\\[\nh(P)&lt;t,\\qquad d(P)\\ge D_Kt\n\\]\ncontains an induced \\((Kt)\\)-connected subgraph of order at most \\(t^{1.06}\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. With the same \\(K,D_K\\), for sufficiently large \\(t\\),\n\\[\nh(P)&lt;t,\\qquad \\ell(P)&gt;2D_Kt+2\n\\]\nalso imply the existence of such an induced \\((Kt)\\)-connected subgraph.</p><p>The cutoffs in the last two assertions may depend on the fixed \\(K\\), but not on the graph.</p>", "proof": "<p>The density, incidence, and density-to-small-district assertions are Lemmas 5.1, 5.2, and 5.4.</p><p>For the list-chromatic consequence, which is original Corollary 5.5, suppose\n\\[\nh(P)&lt;t,\\qquad\\ell(P)&gt;2D_Kt+2.\n\\]\nIf every nonempty subgraph had a vertex of degree at most \\(2D_Kt+1\\), reverse deletion order would color arbitrary lists of size \\(2D_Kt+2\\). Thus some subgraph has minimum degree at least \\(2D_Kt+2\\). Passing to the induced subgraph on its vertices preserves this degree bound, giving density at least \\(D_Kt+1\\). Apply Lemma 5.4 there.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "With \\(C_*=10^4\\), \\( d(P)\\le C_*t\\log(2t). \\) Consequently every graph with \\(h(P)<t\\) satisfies \\( \\ell(P)\\le2C_*t\\log(2t)+1. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(t\\ge2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\) is nonempty and \\(h(P)&lt;t\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(d(P)=e(P)/|V(P)|\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With \\(C_*=10^4\\),\n\\[\nd(P)\\le C_*t\\log(2t).\n\\]\nConsequently every graph with \\(h(P)&lt;t\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\ell(P)\\le2C_*t\\log(2t)+1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a nonempty minor \\(R\\) of \\(P\\) of maximum density \\(d\\), and put \\(n=|V(R)|\\). If \\(d&lt;10^4\\), the claim follows. Assume \\(d\\ge10^4\\).</p><p>There is no isolated vertex in \\(R\\). Contracting an edge \\(xy\\) with \\(c_{xy}\\) common neighbors loses \\(1+c_{xy}\\) edges. Maximality gives\n\\[\n\\frac{dn-1-c_{xy}}{n-1}\\le d,\n\\qquad c_{xy}\\ge d-1.\n\\]\nChoose a vertex \\(v\\) of degree at most \\(2d\\). Then\n\\[\nJ=R[N_R(v)]\n\\]\nhas at most \\(2d\\) vertices and minimum degree at least \\(d-1\\).</p><p>Consider any connected graph \\(C\\) of order at most \\(2d\\) and minimum degree at least \\(d/2\\). Take\n\\[\nm=\\lceil4\\log(2d)\\rceil+1\n\\]\nindependent uniform samples from \\(V(C)\\). A fixed vertex has a neighbor in each draw with probability at least \\(1/4\\), so\n\\[\n\\Pr(\\text{some vertex has no sampled neighbor})\n\\le2d(3/4)^m\n\\le2d e^{-m/4}&lt;1.\n\\]\nThus some set of at most \\(m\\) sampled vertices dominates \\(C\\).</p><p>The diameter of \\(C\\) is at most \\(11\\): along a shortest path, open neighborhoods at positions \\(0,3,6,\\ldots\\) are disjoint, each has at least \\(d/2\\) vertices, and there are at most \\(2d\\) vertices in total. There are at most four such positions. Joining all sampled vertices to one fixed sample by shortest paths gives a connected dominating set of size at most\n\\[\n11m\\le66\\log(2d)\\le M:=\\lceil100\\log(2d)\\rceil.\n\\]\nWe have \\(M\\le101\\log(2d)\\) and \\(d\\ge6M\\), the latter because \\(x/\\log(2x)\\) is increasing for \\(x\\ge10^4\\) and \\(10^4&gt;606\\log(20000)\\).</p><p>Construct\n\\[\nq=\\left\\lfloor\\frac d{3M}\\right\\rfloor\n\\]\npairwise adjacent disjoint connected sets. Start with \\(J_0=J\\). At step \\(i\\), choose a component \\(C_i\\) of \\(J_i\\), choose a connected dominating set \\(B_i\\subseteq V(C_i)\\) of size at most \\(M\\), and put \\(J_{i+1}=C_i-B_i\\).</p><p>Passing to a component deletes no neighbor of any retained vertex. Its missing original neighbors all lie in previously chosen bags. Since at most \\(qM\\le d/3\\) bag vertices are deleted, every retained vertex has degree at least\n\\[\nd-1-qM\\ge d/2.\n\\]\nEach chosen component therefore has at least \\(d/2+1\\) vertices, while \\(M\\le d/6\\), so the construction continues for all \\(q\\) steps.</p><p>Every later bag lies in \\(C_i-B_i\\), and hence has an edge to \\(B_i\\) by domination. Thus\n\\[\nh(P)\\ge q\\ge\\frac d{6M}\n\\ge\\frac d{606\\log(2d)}.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(d/\\log(2d)&lt;606t\\). The inequality \\(\\log(2d)\\le2\\sqrt d\\) first gives\n\\[\nd&lt;(1212t)^2.\n\\]\nSince \\(2\\cdot1212^2&lt;2^{22}\\) and \\(t\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n\\log(2d)&lt;22\\log2+2\\log t\\le12\\log(2t).\n\\]\nHence \\(d&lt;7272t\\log(2t)\\), proving the density bound.</p><p>Every nonempty subgraph of \\(P\\) has average degree at most \\(2C_*t\\log(2t)\\), so it has a vertex of degree at most its floor. Reverse deletion order colors arbitrary lists of size at most the asserted bound. The null graph causes no exception.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For an absolute positive integer \\(B_*\\), \\( \\sum_{\\substack{v\\in T\\\\|N_P(v)\\cap A|\\ge B_*t}} |N_P(v)\\cap A| \\le t^{1+2\\epsilon}|A|, \\) and \\( |\\{v\\in T:|N_P(v)\\cap A|\\ge B_*t\\}| \\le t^{2\\epsilon}|A|. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\epsilon=1/200\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(t\\) is sufficiently large.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(h(P)&lt;t\\), and \\(A,T\\subseteq V(P)\\) are disjoint.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For an absolute positive integer \\(B_*\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{v\\in T\\\\|N_P(v)\\cap A|\\ge B_*t}}\n|N_P(v)\\cap A|\n\\le t^{1+2\\epsilon}|A|,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|\\{v\\in T:|N_P(v)\\cap A|\\ge B_*t\\}|\n\\le t^{2\\epsilon}|A|.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a fixed positive even integer \\(s\\) with \\(\\epsilon s/2&gt;4\\), and put \\(B_*=2s\\).</p><p>First, every set \\(U\\) of size \\(b\\ge B_*t\\) in a graph without a \\(K_t\\) minor contains a vertex with at least \\(t^{-\\epsilon}b\\) nonneighbors in \\(U\\), distinct from itself. Suppose otherwise, and put \\(\\delta=t^{-\\epsilon}\\). Sample an ordered sequence of \\(st\\) distinct vertices of \\(U\\), uniformly, and divide it into \\(t\\) batches of size \\(s\\).</p><p>After any previously exposed positions, a new position is uniform among at least \\(b-st\\ge b/2\\) vertices. For an exposed vertex, the conditional probability the new vertex is a nonneighbor is at most \\(2\\delta\\).</p><p>For a fixed nontrivial cut of one batch, expose a position on the smaller side first, then all positions on the larger side. If the cut has no crossing edge, at least \\(s/2\\) exposures must be nonneighbors of the first vertex. This has probability at most \\((2\\delta)^{s/2}\\). Thus the probability a batch is disconnected is at most \\(2^s(2\\delta)^{s/2}\\).</p><p>For two batches to be anticomplete, every position of the second must be a nonneighbor of one fixed position of the first, an event of probability at most \\((2\\delta)^s\\). The probability of any disconnected batch or anticomplete pair is at most\n\\[\nt2^s(2t^{-\\epsilon})^{s/2}\n+\\binom t2(2t^{-\\epsilon})^s=o(1).\n\\]\nFor large \\(t\\), connected pairwise adjacent batches therefore give a \\(K_t\\) model, a contradiction.</p><p>Let \\(T_0\\) be the vertices in the asserted sum. If \\(A\\) or \\(T_0\\) is empty, there is nothing to prove. Delete all other vertices and all edges within \\(T_0\\). Process \\(T_0\\) one vertex at a time. The graph carried by the vertices representing \\(A\\) only gains edges, and each unprocessed vertex retains its original neighborhood in \\(A\\).</p><p>For \\(v\\in T_0\\), let \\(b_v=|N_P(v)\\cap A|\\). Apply the preceding claim to its neighborhood in the current graph on \\(A\\), a subgraph of a minor of \\(P\\). Choose a vertex with at least \\(t^{-\\epsilon}b_v\\) missing neighbors there, and contract \\(v\\) onto it. At least \\(t^{-\\epsilon}b_v\\) new edges are added to the graph on \\(A\\).</p><p>The final graph on \\(A\\) is a minor of \\(P\\), so Lemma 5.1 gives\n\\[\nt^{-\\epsilon}\\sum_{v\\in T_0}b_v\n\\le e_{\\mathrm{final}}-e(P[A])\n\\le C_*t\\log(2t)|A|.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(t\\), \\(C_*\\log(2t)\\le t^\\epsilon\\). This proves the incidence bound. Dividing by \\(B_*t\\), then using \\(B_*\\ge1\\), gives the cardinality bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma 5.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(P\\) contains an induced \\(k\\)-connected subgraph.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(k\\) is a positive integer.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\) is a nonempty graph with \\(d(P)&gt;2k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">No minor-exclusion assumption is imposed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(P\\) contains an induced \\(k\\)-connected subgraph.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose an induced subgraph \\(H\\) of smallest order \\(v\\ge2k\\) with\n\\[\ne(H)&gt;2k(v-k).\n\\]\nSuch a subgraph exists because the given graph has density greater than \\(2k\\) and more than \\(4k+1\\) vertices. The equality \\(v=2k\\) is impossible since \\(\\binom{2k}{2}&lt;2k^2\\). Minimality therefore gives, for each \\(x\\),\n\\[\n\\deg_H(x)=e(H)-e(H-x)\n&gt;2k(v-k)-2k(v-1-k)=2k.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(H\\) is not \\(k\\)-connected, choose a separator \\(Z\\), \\(|Z|=z&lt;k\\), with nonempty anticomplete sides \\(A,B\\). Each of \\(H[A\\cup Z]\\), \\(H[B\\cup Z]\\) has at least \\(2k\\) vertices and is proper. Minimality gives\n\\[\ne(H)\n=e(H[A\\cup Z])+e(H[B\\cup Z])-e(H[Z])\n\\]\n\\[\n\\le2k(|A|+z-k+|B|+z-k)-e(H[Z])\n\\le2k(v+z-2k)&lt;2k(v-k),\n\\]\na contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "Lemma 5.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(P\\) contains an induced \\((Kt)\\)-connected subgraph of order at most \\(t^{1.06}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(K\\) is a fixed positive integer.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(D_K\\) is an integer with\n\\[\nD_K&gt;\\max(400B_*,12800K).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(t\\) is sufficiently large, \\(P\\) is nonempty, \\(h(P)&lt;t\\), and \\(d(P)\\ge D_Kt\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(P\\) contains an induced \\((Kt)\\)-connected subgraph of order at most \\(t^{1.06}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(n=|V(P)|\\) and \\(d=D_Kt\\). Delete edges to obtain a spanning subgraph \\(P_0\\) with exactly \\(dn\\) edges. Restoring the deleted edges later preserves connectivity on a specified vertex set. Put\n\\[\np_0=\\lfloor t^{5\\epsilon}\\rfloor,\\qquad\n\\lambda=d/100,\n\\]\nand take \\(t\\) large enough that \\(p_0\\ge2\\).</p><p>Choose a family \\(\\mathcal C\\) of as many disjoint connected \\(p_0\\)-vertex sets as possible subject to\n\\[\ne(P_0)-e(P_0/\\mathcal C)\n\\le\\lambda\\sum_{C\\in\\mathcal C}(|C|-1). \\tag{22}\n\\]\nThe empty family is allowed. Let \\(R=P_0/\\mathcal C\\), and let \\(X\\) be the contracted vertices. Vertices outside \\(X\\) are untouched original vertices, with unchanged edges between them. Then\n\\[\n|X|\\le n/p_0,\\qquad\n(d-\\lambda)n\\le e(R)\\le dn. \\tag{23}\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(B\\) be the union of\n\\[\nX,\\quad\n\\{v\\notin X:|N_R(v)\\cap X|\\ge\\lambda/4\\},\\quad\n\\{v:\\deg_R(v)\\ge dt^{4\\epsilon}\\}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\lambda/4=D_Kt/400\\ge B_*t\\), Lemma 5.2 bounds the second set by \\(t^{2\\epsilon}|X|\\). The degree sum bounds the third by \\(2n/t^{4\\epsilon}\\). For sufficiently large \\(t\\),\n\\[\n|B|\n\\le(1+t^{2\\epsilon})n/p_0+2n/t^{4\\epsilon}\n\\le6nt^{-3\\epsilon}. \\tag{24}\n\\]</p><p>Lemma 5.1 bounds edges within \\(B\\) by\n\\[\n6C_*nt^{1-3\\epsilon}\\log(2t).\n\\]\nCrossing edges contributed by outside vertices with fewer than \\(B_*t\\) neighbors in \\(B\\) number at most \\(B_*tn\\). Lemma 5.2 bounds the remaining crossing edges by\n\\[\nt^{1+2\\epsilon}|B|\\le6nt^{1-\\epsilon}.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\ne(R-B)\\ge(d-\\lambda)n\n-6C_*nt^{1-3\\epsilon}\\log(2t)\n-B_*tn-6nt^{1-\\epsilon}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\ge dn\\left(\n\\frac{99}{100}-\\frac{B_*}{D_K}\n-\\frac{6C_*}{D_K}t^{-3\\epsilon}\\log(2t)\n-\\frac6{D_K}t^{-\\epsilon}\n\\right)&gt;dn/2.\n\\]</p><p>Starting with \\(R-B\\), repeatedly delete a vertex whose current degree is less than \\(d/4\\), or less than \\(\\deg_R(v)/8\\). Charge each deleted edge to its first deleted endpoint. The total charge is at most\n\\[\n(d/4)n+\\frac18\\sum_{v\\in V(R)}\\deg_R(v)\\le dn/2.\n\\]\nThus a nonempty set \\(S\\) survives, satisfying\n\\[\n\\deg_{R[S]}(v)\\ge d/4,\\qquad\n\\deg_{R[S]}(v)\\ge\\deg_R(v)/8\n\\quad(v\\in S). \\tag{25}\n\\]</p><p>For a connected nonempty vertex set \\(U\\), let \\(L(U)\\) be the edge loss on contracting \\(U\\). With \\(N_R(U)\\) its external neighborhood,\n\\[\nL(U)=e(R[U])+\n\\sum_{w\\in N_R(U)}(|N_R(w)\\cap U|-1). \\tag{26}\n\\]\nChoose a connected set \\(H\\subseteq S\\) of largest order \\(u\\le p_0\\) subject to\n\\[\nL(H)\\le\\lambda(u-1). \\tag{27}\n\\]\nA singleton is admissible.</p><p>We have \\(u&lt;p_0\\). Otherwise \\(H\\), consisting of untouched original vertices, could be appended to \\(\\mathcal C\\). Contracting \\(\\mathcal C\\) and then \\(H\\) is the same as contracting the enlarged family; edge losses and vertex losses add, contradicting maximality.</p><p>Put \\(N=N_R(H)\\), and\n\\[\na=\\sum_{v\\in H}\\deg_{R[S]}(v),\\qquad\nb=\\sum_{v\\in H}\\deg_R(v).\n\\]\nBy the exact loss formula,\n\\[\na-|N\\cap S|\n=2e(R[H])+\n\\sum_{w\\in N\\cap S}(|N_R(w)\\cap H|-1)\n\\le2L(H)\\le2\\lambda u.\n\\]\nSince \\(a\\ge du/4=25\\lambda u\\), \\(a\\ge b/8\\), and \\(|N|\\le b\\),\n\\[\n|N\\cap S|\n\\ge a-2\\lambda u\n\\ge\\frac{23}{25}a\n\\ge\\frac{23}{200}b\n\\ge |N|/16. \\tag{28}\n\\]\nIn particular \\(N\\cap S\\ne\\varnothing\\).</p><p>Fix \\(w\\in N\\cap S\\), contract \\(H\\) to \\(h\\), and then contract \\(hw\\). The latter contraction loses exactly\n\\[\n1+|N_R(w)\\cap N|\n\\]\nedges. If this were at most \\(\\lambda\\), then \\(H\\cup\\{w\\}\\), of order at most \\(p_0\\), would satisfy the budget, contradicting maximality of \\(u\\). Thus \\(w\\) has more than \\(\\lambda-1\\) neighbors in \\(N\\). Because \\(w\\notin B\\), fewer than \\(\\lambda/4\\) are in \\(X\\). For \\(\\lambda\\ge4\\),\n\\[\n\\deg_{R[N\\setminus X]}(w)&gt;3\\lambda/4-1\\ge\\lambda/2.\n\\]\nUsing \\(N\\cap S\\subseteq N\\setminus X\\),\n\\[\n2e(R[N\\setminus X])\n\\ge(\\lambda/2)|N\\cap S|\n\\ge(\\lambda/32)|N|.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nd(R[N\\setminus X])\n\\ge\\frac{\\lambda|N|}{64|N\\setminus X|}\n\\ge\\lambda/64. \\tag{29}\n\\]</p><p>Every vertex of \\(H\\subseteq S\\) has degree less than \\(dt^{4\\epsilon}\\), so\n\\[\n|N\\setminus X|\n\\le|N|\n&lt;udt^{4\\epsilon}\n\\le D_Kt^{1+9\\epsilon}\n=D_Kt^{1.045}\n\\le t^{1.06}\n\\]\nfor sufficiently large \\(t\\). Also\n\\[\n\\lambda/64=D_Kt/6400&gt;2Kt.\n\\]\nLemma 5.3 gives an induced \\((Kt)\\)-connected subgraph in \\(R[N\\setminus X]\\). Its vertices are untouched original vertices and their edges are exactly those in \\(P_0\\). Restore the deleted edges to obtain the required induced subgraph of \\(P\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Lemmas 6.1–6.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Vertex path packing.** If \\(A,B\\) are disjoint vertex sets in \\(P\\), \\(j\\ge1\\), and every deletion of fewer than \\(j\\) vertices leaves an \\((A\\setminus X)\\)-to-\\((B\\setminus X)\\) path, then there are \\(j\\) disjoint …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All paths are simple. A path with one vertex is permitted when its prescribed ends coincide.</p><p class=\"li\">Unless explicitly permitted to share prescribed starts, path families are vertex-disjoint.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. **Vertex path packing.** If \\(A,B\\) are disjoint vertex sets in \\(P\\), \\(j\\ge1\\), and every deletion of fewer than \\(j\\) vertices leaves an \\((A\\setminus X)\\)-to-\\((B\\setminus X)\\) path, then there are \\(j\\) disjoint paths from distinct vertices of \\(A\\) to distinct vertices of \\(B\\), meeting \\(A\\cup B\\) only at their ends.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. **Two paths per source.** If \\(A,B\\) are disjoint, \\(|A|=r\\ge1\\), \\(P\\) is \\(2r\\)-connected, and \\(|B|\\ge2r\\), there are two paths from each vertex of \\(A\\), ending at \\(2r\\) distinct vertices of \\(B\\), with no intersections except the prescribed repeated starts and with internal vertices outside \\(A\\cup B\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. **Rooting a clique minor.** If \\(P\\) is \\(2k\\)-connected and \\(h(P)\\ge4k\\), then any \\(k\\) distinct prescribed vertices are roots of a \\(K_k\\) model, one in each indexed bag.</p>", "proof": "<p>The three assertions are Lemmas 6.1, 6.2, and 6.3.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are \\(j\\) disjoint paths from distinct vertices of \\(A\\) to distinct vertices of \\(B\\), each meeting \\(A\\cup B\\) only at its ends.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(A,B\\subseteq V(P)\\) are disjoint.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(j\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For every \\(X\\subseteq V(P)\\) with \\(|X|&lt;j\\), \\(P-X\\) contains a path from \\(A\\setminus X\\) to \\(B\\setminus X\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(j\\) disjoint paths from distinct vertices of \\(A\\) to distinct vertices of \\(B\\), each meeting \\(A\\cup B\\) only at its ends.</p>", "proof": "<p>Replace each vertex \\(v\\) by \\(v^-,v^+\\), with an arc \\(v^-v^+\\) of capacity one. For every edge \\(uv\\), add arcs \\(u^+v^-\\), \\(v^+u^-\\), each of capacity \\(j\\). Add a source \\(s\\) with arcs \\(sa^-\\) of capacity \\(j\\), and a sink \\(z\\) with arcs \\(b^+z\\) of capacity \\(j\\).</p><p>Start with zero flow. Whenever the residual graph has an \\(s\\)-to-\\(z\\) path, augment by one unit. Stop upon reaching value \\(j\\), or when no residual path remains. In the latter case let \\(R\\) be the vertices reachable from \\(s\\). Every original arc leaving \\(R\\) is saturated, and every original arc entering \\(R\\) has zero flow. Flow conservation shows that the outgoing cut capacity equals the flow value.</p><p>If that value is less than \\(j\\), no capacity-\\(j\\) arc crosses the cut. Its outgoing arcs are unit arcs \\(v^-v^+\\), corresponding to fewer than \\(j\\) original vertices \\(X\\). An \\(A\\)-to-\\(B\\) path in \\(P-X\\) would give an \\(s\\)-to-\\(z\\) path avoiding these cut arcs, impossible. Hence an integral flow of value \\(j\\) is reached.</p><p>Decompose the flow into unit paths and directed cycles, discarding the cycles. The unit vertex arcs ensure disjointness of the corresponding original paths. On each path retain the segment from its last visit to \\(A\\) preceding its first visit to \\(B\\). This gives the endpoint convention without changing the number or disjointness.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are two paths from each vertex of \\(A\\) to distinct vertices of \\(B\\), all \\(2r\\) target ends distinct, with intersections only at their prescribed repeated starts and internal vertices outside \\(A\\cup B\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(A,B\\subseteq V(P)\\) are disjoint, with \\(|A|=r\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\) is \\(2r\\)-connected and \\(|B|\\ge2r\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are two paths from each vertex of \\(A\\) to distinct vertices of \\(B\\), all \\(2r\\) target ends distinct, with intersections only at their prescribed repeated starts and internal vertices outside \\(A\\cup B\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Replace each vertex of \\(A\\) by two copies. For each original edge, join every copy of one endpoint to every copy of the other, treating vertices outside \\(A\\) as single copies. Let \\(A^*\\) be the \\(2r\\) source copies.</p><p>Delete fewer than \\(2r\\) vertices \\(X^*\\) of the copy graph. A source copy and a target survive. In the original graph delete the vertices all of whose copies were deleted; this set \\(X\\) has size at most \\(|X^*|&lt;2r\\). The surviving original source and target are connected in \\(P-X\\), and a simple path lifts by choosing surviving copies. Thus \\(X^*\\) cannot separate \\(A^*\\) from \\(B\\).</p><p>Lemma 6.1 gives \\(2r\\) disjoint paths from \\(A^*\\) to \\(B\\). Every source copy is an endpoint and no source copy is internal. Identifying the two copies of each source introduces exactly the permitted repeated starts. The endpoint convention excludes internal vertices in \\(B\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma 6.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a \\(K_k\\) model with bags indexed by \\(R\\), each containing its indexing root.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(k\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\) is \\(2k\\)-connected and \\(h(P)\\ge4k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(R\\) is a prescribed set of \\(k\\) distinct vertices.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a \\(K_k\\) model with bags indexed by \\(R\\), each containing its indexing root.</p>", "proof": "<p>Start with \\(4k\\) bags of a clique model. At most \\(k\\) meet \\(R\\), so choose \\(3k\\) bags disjoint from \\(R\\), called eligible, and one representative in each. Lemma 6.2 gives two paths per root with ends in \\(2k\\) distinct eligible bags.</p><p>Among such double systems, allowing arbitrary endpoints in the eligible bags but retaining distinct end bags, choose one of minimum total length. No path meets an eligible bag that is not an end bag: its first such visit would permit truncation and assignment to that unused bag, reducing total length. Therefore at least \\(k\\) eligible bags are untouched. Contract \\(k\\) of them to targets \\(T\\).</p><p>For each of the \\(2k\\) paths, take its union with its own end bag. These connected sets are unchanged by the target contractions. Every vertex belongs to at most two: a nonroot belongs to at most one path and at most one eligible bag; a root belongs to its two paths and no eligible bag. This allows paths to pass through other used end bags.</p><p>Deleting fewer than \\(k\\) vertices in the contracted graph meets fewer than \\(2k\\) of these sets, so one survives intact, including its root. A target also survives. The surviving set has an edge to every surviving target, by the original clique-model adjacencies. Thus these deletions cannot separate \\(R\\) from \\(T\\).</p><p>Lemma 6.1 with \\(j=k\\) gives disjoint root-to-target paths, using all roots and targets and none internally. Expand each target bag and add its root-to-target path. The resulting bags are connected, disjoint, rooted as required, and remain pairwise adjacent through the original target-bag edges.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Definition 6.4, Lemma 6.5, Remark 6.6, and Lemma 6.7", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\(J\\) is an \\((a,b)\\)-woven subgraph of \\(P\\), \\(a\\) roots are prescribed in \\(J\\), and \\(Q_1,\\ldots,Q_q\\) are disjoint paths with \\(q\\le b\\), then one can reroute the paths through \\(J\\), preserving their respective …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For integers \\(a\\ge1\\), \\(b\\ge0\\), a graph \\(J\\) is \\((a,b)\\)-woven if the following holds.</p><p class=\"li\">Prescribe distinct roots \\(R=\\{r_1,\\ldots,r_a\\}\\) and at most \\(b\\) pairs \\((x_i,y_i)\\). Endpoint sets of different pairs are disjoint; \\(x_i=y_i\\) is allowed, and endpoints may belong to \\(R\\).</p><p class=\"li\">There are a \\(K_a\\) model \\(M_1,\\ldots,M_a\\), with \\(r_j\\in M_j\\), and disjoint \\(x_i\\)-to-\\(y_i\\) paths \\(Q_i\\), such that\n\\[\nV(Q_i)\\cap\\bigcup_{j=1}^aM_j\n\\subseteq R\\cap\\{x_i,y_i\\}.\n\\]\nFor a coincident pair the path is the single vertex.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the constant \\(\\gamma\\) in Proposition 1, and set\n\\[\nK_0=10^5\\gamma. \\tag{30}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(J\\) is an \\((a,b)\\)-woven subgraph of \\(P\\), \\(a\\) roots are prescribed in \\(J\\), and \\(Q_1,\\ldots,Q_q\\) are disjoint paths with \\(q\\le b\\), then one can reroute the paths through \\(J\\), preserving their respective endpoints, while constructing the rooted model in \\(J\\). If \\(x_i,y_i\\) are the first and last visits of an oriented path to \\(J\\), then the rerouted paths \\(Q'_i\\) satisfy:\n\\[\nV(Q'_i)\\setminus V(J)\\subseteq V(Q_i)\\setminus V(J);\n\\]\npaths disjoint from \\(J\\) are unchanged; and the model intersection with \\(Q'_i\\) is contained in the prescribed roots among \\(x_i,y_i\\). This may be applied successively in disjoint districts.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For all sufficiently large \\(t\\), every \\((K_0t)\\)-connected graph \\(J\\) of order at most \\(t^{1.06}\\) is \\((s,3t)\\)-woven whenever\n\\[\n1\\le s\\le2\\left\\lceil\\frac{t}{(\\log t)^2}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nNo minor-exclusion assumption is required, and the cutoff is uniform in \\(s,J\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The replacement assertion is Lemma 7.1, including the stated endpoint-intersection conclusions. The small-district assertion is Lemma 7.2.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are disjoint paths \\(Q'_i\\) with the same respective endpoints and a model rooted at the prescribed roots in \\(J\\), such that:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(J\\subseteq P\\) is \\((a,b)\\)-woven, with \\(a\\) prescribed distinct roots in \\(J\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Q_1,\\ldots,Q_q\\) are disjoint paths in \\(P\\), where \\(q\\le b\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Orient each path. For every path meeting \\(J\\), let \\(x_i,y_i\\) be its first and last vertices in \\(J\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are disjoint paths \\(Q'_i\\) with the same respective endpoints and a model rooted at the prescribed roots in \\(J\\), such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(V(Q'_i)\\setminus V(J)\\subseteq V(Q_i)\\setminus V(J)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. A path originally disjoint from \\(J\\) is unchanged.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The intersection of \\(Q'_i\\) with the model is contained in the prescribed roots among \\(x_i,y_i\\).</p><p>The construction applies successively in disjoint districts. A bag whose root is an original path endpoint meets the resulting paths only at its own root, also after subsequent replacements in other districts.</p>", "proof": "<p>The pairs \\((x_i,y_i)\\) are admissible for wovenness: their endpoint sets are disjoint across paths, and a single visit gives an allowed coincident pair. Apply wovenness to these pairs.</p><p>Replace each segment from \\(x_i\\) to \\(y_i\\) by its supplied path in \\(J\\). The retained prefix and suffix have no vertices in \\(J\\) except their attachment endpoints. Retained pieces of distinct original paths are disjoint, as are the replacement paths. Thus the concatenations are simple and disjoint and have the required endpoints and intersections. Excursions outside \\(J\\) in the replaced segment are removed, giving inclusion rather than equality outside \\(J\\).</p><p>At a later replacement, new vertices belong to a district disjoint from every earlier district and model, so no new intersection with an earlier model is introduced. An original path endpoint remains that endpoint and is a first or last visit in its own district. No other path contains it, so its bag has precisely the permitted intersection.</p><p>As recorded in original Remark 6.6, not every prescribed root need be a path endpoint. An auxiliary root may be a first or last visit of an unrelated path, and its bag may then meet that path there. Discarding auxiliary bags removes this issue when only endpoint-rooted bags are retained. A root strictly between the first and last visits need not remain on the path after rerouting.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.7", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(J\\) is \\((s,3t)\\)-woven.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(K_0=10^5\\gamma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(t\\) is sufficiently large.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(J\\) is \\((K_0t)\\)-connected and \\(|V(J)|\\le t^{1.06}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(1\\le s\\le2\\lceil t/(\\log t)^2\\rceil\\).</p><p class=\"li\">No minor-exclusion assumption is imposed on \\(J\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(J\\) is \\((s,3t)\\)-woven.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix an admissible root set and at most \\(3t\\) pairs. Let \\(Z\\) be the union of roots and pair endpoints. For sufficiently large \\(t\\), \\(s\\le t\\), so \\(|Z|\\le7t\\). Put\n\\[\nk'=\\lfloor K_0t/2\\rfloor.\n\\]\nDeleting \\(Z\\) leaves a \\(k'\\)-connected graph, and every vertex of \\(J\\), including those in \\(Z\\), has at least \\(k'\\) neighbors outside \\(Z\\).</p><p>Independently place each vertex of \\(J-Z\\) in \\(A\\) with probability \\(1/1000\\), and let\n\\[\nB'=V(J)\\setminus(Z\\cup A).\n\\]\nWith probability tending to one,\n\\[\n|N_J(v)\\cap A|\\ge k'/2000,\\qquad\n|N_J(v)\\cap B'|\\ge k'/2\n\\quad(v\\in V(J)), \\tag{31}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nJ[B']\\text{ is }\\lfloor k'/32\\rfloor\\text{-connected}. \\tag{32}\n\\]</p><p>For the degree assertions, fix \\(k'\\) neighbors outside \\(Z\\) for each vertex. Their number in \\(A\\) has mean \\(k'/1000\\). Proposition 1 bounds the probability it is below \\(k'/2000\\) or above \\(k'/2\\) by \\(\\exp(-\\Omega(k'))\\). A union bound over at most \\(t^{1.06}\\) vertices applies.</p><p>For connectivity, fix the cut-test matchings of Proposition 1 for \\(J-Z\\). There are at most\n\\[\n\\exp(O(t^{0.06}(\\log t)^2))\n\\]\ntests. A test has at most \\(k'/4\\) endpoints and expected intersection with \\(A\\) at most \\(k'/4000\\). Reaching \\(k'/32\\) requires a fixed positive multiple of \\(k'\\) as deviation. The bounded-differences estimate gives failure probability \\(\\exp(-\\Omega(k'))\\) per test. On the degree event, every vertex of \\(J-Z\\) has at least \\(k'/2\\) neighbors outside \\(A\\). Thus the cut test applies with positive probability. Fix such a split.</p><p>The graph \\(J[A]\\) has minimum degree at least \\(k'/2000\\), hence density at least \\(k'/4000=\\Omega(t)\\). Uniformly for allowed \\(s\\ge2\\),\n\\[\ns\\log(2s)=O(t/\\log t)=o(t).\n\\]\nThe density bound in Proposition 5 therefore forces a \\(K_s\\) model in \\(J[A]\\). For \\(s=1\\), use a vertex of \\(A\\). Denote its bags by \\(M^0_1,\\ldots,M^0_s\\), and select ports \\(p_j\\in M^0_j\\).</p><p>Choose distinct neighboring representatives in \\(B'\\) for every port, every root, and both endpoints of each noncoincident pair. A root that is also a pair endpoint gets different representatives for the two roles. There are at most \\(2s+6t\\le8t\\) roles, each with at least \\(k'/2\\) available neighbors.</p><p>Group the port and root representatives for each label, and group the two representatives for each noncoincident pair. Since\n\\[\n\\lfloor k'/32\\rfloor\\ge\\gamma(2s+6t),\n\\]\nthe grouping input in Proposition 1 gives disjoint connected sets in \\(J[B']\\). Let \\(C_j\\) join the representatives of \\(p_j,r_j\\). Then\n\\[\nM_j=M_j^0\\cup C_j\\cup\\{r_j\\}\n\\]\nis connected using the attachment edges. These bags are disjoint and retain pairwise adjacencies from the model in \\(A\\).</p><p>For each other group, choose a path between its representatives and attach the original endpoints. Use the trivial path for a coincident pair. The resulting paths are disjoint, and the model contains no vertex of \\(Z\\) except its own roots. Thus the only model–path intersections are the allowed root endpoints.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemmas 6.8 and 6.9", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If a feasible system of either type is chosen with minimum total length, and \\(U\\) is its vertex union, then:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Path length means number of edges.</p><p class=\"li\">For disjoint source and target sets \\(A,B\\), consider:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. two paths from every vertex of \\(A\\), with distinct target ends in \\(B\\), intersections permitted only at prescribed repeated starts; or</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. a specified number \\(q\\) of disjoint paths from arbitrary distinct vertices of \\(A\\) to arbitrary distinct vertices of \\(B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">No pairing of sources and targets is prescribed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. If a feasible system of either type is chosen with minimum total length, and \\(U\\) is its vertex union, then:</p><p class=\"li l2\">its paths have no internal vertices in \\(A\\cup B\\);</p><p class=\"li l2\">every vertex outside \\(U\\) has at most three neighbors on any one path;</p><p class=\"li l2\">for type 1, \\(P[U\\setminus A]\\) is \\(2q\\)-degenerate, and for type 2, \\(P[U]\\) is \\(2q\\)-degenerate.\n   All these conclusions concern edges of the graph in which the minimization is performed.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Suppose \\(Z,D,V(H)\\) are pairwise disjoint, \\(m\\ge1\\), and there are:</p><p class=\"li l2\">two paths from each vertex of \\(Z\\) to distinct vertices of \\(D\\), all target ends distinct, intersecting only at repeated starts and avoiding \\(H\\);</p><p class=\"li l2\">\\(2m\\) disjoint paths from distinct vertices of \\(H\\) to distinct vertices of \\(D\\), avoiding \\(Z\\) and meeting \\(H\\) only at their starts.\n   \n   Using only the two families, there are \\(|Z|+m\\) disjoint paths to distinct vertices of \\(D\\), one from each vertex of \\(Z\\) and one from each of \\(m\\) distinct vertices of \\(H\\). They meet \\(D,H\\) only at the appropriate ends. After any partition of the \\(2m\\) second-family starts into \\(m\\) pairs, exactly one start from each pair can be selected, and no unselected start is visited.</p>", "proof": "<p>The minimizing-system conclusions are Lemma 8.1. The combination conclusion is Lemma 8.2.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The paths have no internal vertices in \\(A\\cup B\\); every \\(v\\notin U\\) has at most three neighbors on each path; and \\(P[U\\setminus A]\\) is \\(2q\\)-degenerate for type 1, while \\(P[U]\\) is \\(2q\\)-degenerate for type 2.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(A,B\\) are disjoint vertex sets in \\(P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A system of one of the two types defined in Proposition 8 is feasible and is chosen with minimum total length.</p><p class=\"li\">Its number of paths is \\(q\\), and its vertex union is \\(U\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The paths have no internal vertices in \\(A\\cup B\\); every \\(v\\notin U\\) has at most three neighbors on each path; and \\(P[U\\setminus A]\\) is \\(2q\\)-degenerate for type 1, while \\(P[U]\\) is \\(2q\\)-degenerate for type 2.</p>", "proof": "<p>An internal visit to \\(B\\) permits truncation, with a new target not used by another path. For type 2, an internal visit to \\(A\\) similarly permits deleting the initial segment. For type 1, all sources are prescribed starts and the intersection rule excludes internal visits to \\(A\\).</p><p>For the neighbor bound, suppose \\(v\\notin U\\) has at least four neighbors on\n\\[\nx_0x_1\\cdots x_l,\n\\]\noriented from source to target. The first and last neighbor indices \\(i&lt;j\\) satisfy \\(j-i\\ge3\\). If \\(v\\notin A\\cup B\\), replace the segment from \\(x_i\\) to \\(x_j\\) by \\(x_ivx_j\\), saving an edge. If \\(v\\in B\\), truncate after \\(x_i\\) and use \\(v\\) as target; its length is \\(i+1\\le l-2\\). If \\(v\\in A\\), the system has type 2, and one can start at \\(v\\) and follow the suffix from \\(x_j\\), with length \\(1+l-j\\le l-2\\). Each contradicts minimality.</p><p>For degeneracy, put \\(U_0=U\\setminus A\\) in type 1 and \\(U_0=U\\) in type 2. Suppose some nonempty \\(P[S]\\), \\(S\\subseteq U_0\\), has minimum degree greater than \\(2q\\). On each path meeting \\(S\\), mark its first two vertices in \\(S\\), or its only vertex if there is one. At most \\(2q\\) vertices are marked. Every vertex of \\(S\\) lies on exactly one path.</p><p>For each participating path \\(Q_i\\), let \\(u_i\\) be its first vertex in \\(S\\). It has a neighbor \\(v_i\\in S\\) that is unmarked, hence at least the third vertex in \\(S\\) on some path \\(Q_j\\). Direct an arrow \\(i\\to j\\), recording \\(u_iv_i\\). Every participating path has an outgoing arrow, so there is a directed cycle, possibly a loop.</p><p>For every arrow \\(i\\to j\\) on the cycle, concatenate the prefix of \\(Q_i\\) ending at \\(u_i\\), the edge \\(u_iv_i\\), and the suffix of \\(Q_j\\) starting at \\(v_i\\). Leave all other paths unchanged. On each cycle path, its retained prefix ends at its first \\(S\\)-vertex and its retained suffix starts at least at its third \\(S\\)-vertex. The pieces are disjoint and separated by at least two original edges. Pieces on distinct original paths are disjoint except for permitted repeated starts. Thus the new paths satisfy the original intersection conditions.</p><p>All source prefixes are retained, including both prefixes of a repeated source. Target suffixes are permuted, which is permitted because no pairing was prescribed. If the cycle has \\(c\\) paths, at least \\(2c\\) original edges are removed and exactly \\(c\\) joining edges inserted. Total length decreases, a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.9", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Using only these families, there are \\(|Z|+m\\) disjoint paths to distinct vertices of \\(D\\), one from each vertex of \\(Z\\) and one from exactly one start in each prescribed pair.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(Z,D,V(H)\\) are pairwise disjoint and \\(m\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A first family has two paths per vertex of \\(Z\\), distinct target ends in \\(D\\), intersections only at repeated starts, and avoids \\(H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A second family has \\(2m\\) disjoint paths from distinct vertices of \\(H\\) to distinct vertices of \\(D\\), avoids \\(Z\\), and meets \\(H\\) only at starts.</p><p class=\"li\">The second-family starts are partitioned into \\(m\\) prescribed pairs.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Using only these families, there are \\(|Z|+m\\) disjoint paths to distinct vertices of \\(D\\), one from each vertex of \\(Z\\) and one from exactly one start in each prescribed pair. They meet \\(D,H\\) only at appropriate ends and do not visit any unselected second-family start.</p>", "proof": "<p>Truncate each input path at its first visit to \\(D\\). Work only in the graph of the two families. Identify the two vertices of each prescribed pair of second-family starts, obtaining a temporary source. Since the second family has no other vertices in \\(H\\), and the first avoids \\(H\\), every input path remains a path.</p><p>Put \\(N=|Z|+m\\). There are \\(N\\) sources, each with two routes to \\(D\\), and every vertex has congestion at most two among these \\(2N\\) routes. At a vertex of \\(Z\\) only its two first-family routes occur; at a temporary source only its two second-family routes occur; elsewhere there is at most one route from each family. This includes target vertices.</p><p>Deleting fewer than \\(N\\) vertices meets fewer than \\(2N\\) routes, so an entire source-to-\\(D\\) route survives. Proposition 6 gives \\(N\\) disjoint paths from all sources to \\(D\\), without internal sources or targets.</p><p>Each temporary source occurs only as a starting endpoint. Lift the first edge of its packed path to whichever original start supplied that edge, keeping the rest unchanged. This chooses exactly one start per pair without switching between the originals. The lifted paths remain disjoint.</p><p>The only vertices of \\(H\\) in the original family graph were the \\(2m\\) starts, whose images were sources and could not be internal. Hence the lifted paths meet \\(H\\) exactly at their selected starts and avoid all unselected starts.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Lemma 7.2, deleting a history of paths", "depth": 0, "snippet": "With \\( c_f=c_{6B_*+20}=4L+6B_*+25, \\) every final footprint satisfies \\( \\ell(G-F_{\\mathrm{last}})\\ge\\ell(G)-c_ft. \\) The assertion also holds for the initial footprint, and its cutoff is independent of the history and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(h(G)&lt;t\\), and \\(t\\) is sufficiently large.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nm_0=\\lceil(\\log t)^2\\rceil,\\qquad\nR_t=m_0(m_0+1)/2.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Begin with \\(F_0=W_0\\), \\(|W_0|=t\\), and perform at most \\(R_t\\) rounds.</p><p class=\"li\">At the start of a round, \\(F\\) is the footprint and \\(W\\subseteq F\\) is a set of \\(t\\) tips.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose disjoint \\(J,D\\subseteq V(G)\\setminus F\\), each of size at most \\(t^{1.06}\\), and \\(R\\subseteq J\\), \\(|R|\\le t\\). Put \\(Z=W\\cup R\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In the induced graph \\(G-(F\\setminus W)\\), choose a minimum-total-length double system \\(\\mathcal Q_1\\) from \\(Z\\) to \\(D\\), two paths per source, otherwise disjoint and with distinct target ends. Put\n\\[\nF^-=F\\cup J\\cup D\\cup V(\\mathcal Q_1).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(A\\subseteq V(G)\\setminus F^-\\). In \\(G-(F\\cup R)\\), choose a minimum-total-length system \\(\\mathcal Q_2\\) of \\(2t\\) disjoint paths from arbitrary distinct vertices of \\(A\\) to arbitrary distinct vertices of \\(D\\). Put\n\\[\nF^+=F^-\\cup V(\\mathcal Q_2),\n\\]\nand choose \\(t\\) tips \\(W^+\\subseteq F^+\\) for the next round.</p><p class=\"li\">Only histories in which the required systems exist are considered. The last round may stop after its first system.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With\n\\[\nc_f=c_{6B_*+20}=4L+6B_*+25, \\tag{33}\n\\]\nevery final footprint satisfies\n\\[\n\\ell(G-F_{\\mathrm{last}})\\ge\\ell(G)-c_ft.\n\\]\nThe assertion also holds for the initial footprint, and its cutoff is independent of the history and path lengths.</p>", "proof": "<p>Remove the start of each first-system path, and collect all nonempty resulting paths in \\(\\mathcal A\\). These paths are pairwise disjoint: within a round only repeated starts were shared; across rounds a new first system avoids the old footprint except at tips, which are starts and are removed.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal B\\) consist of the full second-system paths. These are also pairwise disjoint, because each new second system avoids the old footprint. No disjointness between \\(\\mathcal A\\) and \\(\\mathcal B\\) is required. If \\(q\\le R_t\\) rounds have begun, then\n\\[\n|\\mathcal A|+|\\mathcal B|\\le6tq\\le6tR_t. \\tag{34}\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(X_0\\) contain all districts, all tip sets appearing in the history, and every path end. Counting repetitions only increases the bound\n\\[\n|X_0|\\le2R_tt^{1.06}+(13R_t+1)t. \\tag{35}\n\\]\nThere are at most \\(R_t+1\\) tip sets and at most \\(12t\\) path-end occurrences per round.</p><p>Take as snapshots the footprints at the start of each round and the final footprint. For a snapshot \\(T\\), let \\(\\mathcal A_T,\\mathcal B_T\\) be the paths already included, with only first-path starts removed. For either collection \\(\\mathcal C\\), declare \\(v\\notin T\\) exceptional if it has a neighbor on at least \\(B_*t\\) distinct members of \\(\\mathcal C\\).</p><p>Contract the disjoint connected paths of \\(\\mathcal C\\). Vertices outside \\(T\\) remain uncontracted, and their numbers of neighbors in the contracted set equal the numbers of paths they see. Proposition 5 bounds the number of exceptional vertices by\n\\[\n\\frac{t^{1+2\\epsilon}|\\mathcal C|}{B_*t}\n\\le t^{2\\epsilon}|\\mathcal C|.\n\\]\nFor both collections together, the number at one snapshot is at most \\(6R_tt^{1+2\\epsilon}\\).</p><p>Let \\(X\\) be \\(X_0\\) together with all exceptional vertices at all snapshots, including those outside \\(F_{\\mathrm{last}}\\). Then\n\\[\n|X|\\le2R_tt^{1.06}+(13R_t+1)t\n+6R_t(R_t+1)t^{1+2\\epsilon}\n\\]\n\\[\n=O(t^{1.06}\\log^4t)+O(t^{1.01}\\log^8t)\n\\le t^{11/10}. \\tag{36}\n\\]\nPut \\(Y=F_{\\mathrm{last}}\\setminus X\\). Every vertex of \\(Y\\) lies on a recorded path.</p><p>If \\(v\\notin X\\cup Y\\), then \\(v\\notin F_{\\mathrm{last}}\\), so it lies outside every path union and belongs to every graph in which a system was minimized. Proposition 8 bounds its neighbors on each path by three. Nonexceptionality at the final snapshot bounds the number of paths it sees by fewer than \\(B_*t\\) in each collection. All stripped starts lie in \\(X_0\\). Thus\n\\[\n|N_G(v)\\cap Y|\\le6B_*t. \\tag{37}\n\\]</p><p>To bound degeneracy, order \\(Y\\) by first-entry round. Within each round, put first-system vertices first and the remaining new second-system vertices second. Districts and initial tips lie in \\(X_0\\), so this partitions \\(Y\\) into at most two phases per round.</p><p>A vertex entering a round is outside its old footprint \\(F\\). If it lies in \\(Y\\), it is not exceptional at snapshot \\(F\\). For every earlier system it was in the minimization graph and outside the path union. Hence it has at most \\(6B_*t\\) neighbors in \\(Y\\) from earlier rounds.</p><p>A first phase is contained in at most \\(4t\\) first-system paths with starts removed, so Proposition 8 makes its induced graph \\(8t\\)-degenerate. A second phase lies in \\(2t\\) minimizing second-system paths, so it is \\(4t\\)-degenerate, hence \\(8t\\)-degenerate. These are induced-graph assertions in \\(G\\), since all phase vertices belong to the corresponding induced minimization graph.</p><p>A second-phase vertex lies outside the entire first-system union and outside the old footprint, so Proposition 8 bounds its neighbors in the first phase of that round by \\(3(4t)=12t\\).</p><p>In any nonempty subgraph of \\(G[Y]\\), choose the latest represented phase and a vertex of degree at most \\(8t\\) within that phase. It has at most \\(12t\\) neighbors in an earlier phase of the same round, and at most \\(6B_*t\\) in earlier rounds. Therefore \\(G[Y]\\) is \\((6B_*+20)t\\)-degenerate.</p><p>Equations (36) and (37) verify Proposition 4 with \\(d=6B_*+20\\), giving\n\\[\n\\ell(G-X-Y)\\ge\\ell(G)-c_ft.\n\\]\nSince \\(F_{\\mathrm{last}}\\subseteq X\\cup Y\\), monotonicity in Proposition 3 proves the desired inequality. For the initial footprint, use \\(Y=\\varnothing\\), \\(X=W_0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Lemma 7.1, separability", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an absolute positive integer \\(S\\) such that, whenever \\(r\\ge2St\\), there are vertex-disjoint induced subgraphs \\(G_1,G_2\\) satisfying \\( \\ell(G_1),\\ell(G_2)\\ge r-St. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(G\\) is a finite graph and \\(t\\) is sufficiently large.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(h(G)&lt;t\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(r=\\ell(G)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The constants \\(L,K_0,B_*,D_{K_0},c_f\\) are those fixed in earlier propositions.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an absolute positive integer \\(S\\) such that, whenever \\(r\\ge2St\\), there are vertex-disjoint induced subgraphs \\(G_1,G_2\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\ell(G_1),\\ell(G_2)\\ge r-St.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(k=K_0t\\), and define\n\\[\nc_h=c_f+6(K_0+1)+2. \\tag{38}\n\\]\nLet \\(c_0=4L+5\\), the constant in Proposition 4 for \\(d=0\\). Choose a positive integer \\(S\\) such that\n\\[\nS&gt;c_h+K_0+2,\n\\]\n\\[\n2S-c_f&gt;6(K_0+1)+2,\n\\]\n\\[\n2S-c_h-1-c_0&gt;2D_{K_0}+3. \\tag{39}\n\\]\nTake \\(t\\) sufficiently large for the earlier propositions with these fixed parameters.</p><p>Suppose \\(r\\ge2St\\), \\(h(G)&lt;t\\), but no two disjoint induced subgraphs both have list chromatic number at least \\(r-St\\). We construct a \\(K_t\\) model.</p><p>Put \\(m_0=\\lceil(\\log t)^2\\rceil\\). Partition the \\(t\\) labels into \\(m_0\\) nonempty blocks, each of size at most \\(\\lceil t/m_0\\rceil\\). Schedule one round for each block and one for each unordered pair of blocks, a total of \\(R_t=m_0(m_0+1)/2\\) rounds. The labels treated in one round form \\(I\\), of size \\(s\\), where\n\\[\n1\\le s\\le2\\lceil t/m_0\\rceil\n\\le2\\left\\lceil\\frac t{(\\log t)^2}\\right\\rceil\n\\le t. \\tag{40}\n\\]\nEvery pair of labels occurs together in a scheduled round.</p><p>At the start of each round maintain:</p><p class=\"li\">disjoint nonempty connected bags \\(B_1,\\ldots,B_t\\), retaining all earlier adjacencies;</p><p class=\"li\">a footprint \\(F\\) of the form in Proposition 9, containing the bags and all earlier districts and original minimizing path systems;</p><p class=\"li\">an induced \\(k\\)-connected region \\(H\\), with\n\\[\n\\ell(H)\\ge r-c_ht,\\qquad V(H)\\cap F=W,\n\\]\nwhere \\(W=\\{w_1,\\ldots,w_t\\}\\) consists of distinct tips, \\(w_j\\in B_j\\).</p><p>Initially \\(r&gt;6k\\), so Proposition 3 supplies \\(H\\) with \\(\\ell(H)\\ge r-6k\\ge r-c_ht\\). Choose \\(t\\) distinct tips, and put \\(B_j=\\{w_j\\}\\), \\(F=W\\).</p><p>In a round,\n\\[\n\\ell(H-W)\\ge r-(c_h+1)t.\n\\]\nProposition 5 supplies an induced \\(k\\)-connected district \\(J\\subseteq H-W\\) of order at most \\(t^{1.06}\\). Delete it by Proposition 4, losing at most \\(c_0t\\). The remainder has list chromatic number at least\n\\[\nr-(c_h+1+c_0)t\n\\ge(2S-c_h-1-c_0)t\n&gt;(2D_{K_0}+3)t&gt;2D_{K_0}t+2.\n\\]\nProposition 5 supplies a second induced \\(k\\)-connected district \\(D\\subseteq H-W-J\\), also of order at most \\(t^{1.06}\\). Both are disjoint from \\(F\\).</p><p>Choose roots \\(R_J=\\{u_j:j\\in I\\}\\subseteq J\\), and put \\(Z=W\\cup R_J\\), so \\(|Z|=t+s\\le2t\\). The graph \\(H\\) is \\(2|Z|\\)-connected and contains \\(Z,D\\), with \\(|D|&gt;k\\ge2|Z|\\). Proposition 6 gives a double system. Minimize its total length in the full induced graph\n\\[\nG-(F\\setminus W). \\tag{41}\n\\]\nCall it \\(\\mathcal Q_1\\), and put\n\\[\nF^-=F\\cup V(J)\\cup V(D)\\cup V(\\mathcal Q_1).\n\\]\nProposition 9 applies to this history and gives\n\\[\n\\ell(G-F^-)\\ge r-c_ft. \\tag{42}\n\\]</p><p>By (39), this exceeds \\(6(K_0+1)t\\). Proposition 3 supplies an induced \\((K_0+1)t\\)-connected\n\\[\nH'\\subseteq G-F^-\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n\\ell(H')\\ge r-(c_f+6(K_0+1))t. \\tag{43}\n\\]</p><p>We claim \\(|V(H')\\cap V(H)|\\ge k\\). Otherwise deleting the intersection from \\(H'\\) leaves a graph disjoint from \\(H\\) of list chromatic number at least\n\\[\nr-(c_f+6(K_0+1)+K_0)t\\ge r-St.\n\\]\nAlso \\(\\ell(H)\\ge r-c_ht\\ge r-St\\), contrary to the supposition.</p><p>Thus the induced union of \\(H,H'\\) is \\(k\\)-connected: after fewer than \\(k\\) deletions both regions are connected and an intersection vertex survives. It meets \\(F\\) only in \\(W\\). Deleting \\(Z\\) leaves connectivity at least\n\\[\nk-|Z|\\ge(K_0-2)t\\ge2t\n\\]\ninside\n\\[\nG-(F\\cup R_J). \\tag{44}\n\\]\nThe disjoint sets \\(H',D\\) avoid \\(Z\\) and each have more than \\(2t\\) vertices. Proposition 6 gives \\(2t\\) disjoint paths between them.</p><p>Minimize total length over all \\(2t\\)-path systems with freely chosen distinct starts in \\(H'\\) and targets in \\(D\\), in the full graph (44). Call the system \\(\\mathcal Q_2\\), with start set \\(T'\\subseteq V(H')\\), \\(|T'|=2t\\), and put\n\\[\nF^+=F^-\\cup V(\\mathcal Q_2).\n\\]\nBy Proposition 8, these paths meet \\(H',D\\) only at their ends, so\n\\[\nV(H')\\cap F^+=T'. \\tag{45}\n\\]\nThis is another round of the history in Proposition 9.</p><p>The first system avoids \\(H'\\), and the second avoids \\(Z\\). Proposition 8 combines them into\n\\[\nq=|Z|+t=2t+s\\le3t\n\\]\ndisjoint paths to distinct vertices of \\(D\\), one from each vertex of \\(Z\\) and one from each vertex of a selected \\(W^+\\subseteq T'\\), \\(|W^+|=t\\). They meet \\(D,H'\\) only at designated ends, avoid \\(T'\\setminus W^+\\), and meet \\(F\\) only at the \\(W\\)-ends. Assign \\(W^+=\\{w_1^+,\\ldots,w_t^+\\}\\) bijectively to labels.</p><p>By Proposition 7 and (40), \\(J\\) is \\((s,3t)\\)-woven. Reroute the combined paths through \\(J\\), obtaining a rooted \\(K_s\\) model with bags \\(C_j\\), \\(j\\in I\\). Each \\(u_j\\) is its own path endpoint, so the model meets the paths only at the corresponding roots. New vertices lie in \\(J\\subseteq F^+\\); as \\(J\\) avoids \\(F,H',D\\), the previous endpoint-only intersection statements persist. The original minimizing systems, not the rerouted paths, remain recorded in the history.</p><p>For each label \\(j\\), group in \\(D\\) the terminals of the paths from \\(w_j,w_j^+\\), and, if \\(j\\in I\\), \\(u_j\\). Since \\(\\gamma q\\le3\\gamma t\\le K_0t\\), Proposition 1 supplies disjoint connected sets \\(E_1,\\ldots,E_t\\) realizing these groups.</p><p>Extend \\(B_j\\) by its two or three paths, \\(E_j\\), and \\(C_j\\) when \\(j\\in I\\). The resulting \\(B_j^+\\) is connected and contains \\(w_j^+\\). Different extended bags are disjoint: old bags meet added paths only at their own tips; paths are disjoint; grouping sets meet paths only at their terminals; and the model bags meet paths only at their own roots. All lie in \\(F^+\\). Old adjacencies persist, and every pair of labels in \\(I\\) gains an adjacency through the district model.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nH^+=H'-(T'\\setminus W^+).\n\\]\nExactly \\(t\\) vertices are deleted, so \\(H^+\\) is \\(K_0t\\)-connected. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\ell(H^+)\n\\ge r-(c_f+6(K_0+1)+1)t\n\\ge r-c_ht,\n\\]\nand (45) gives \\(V(H^+)\\cap F^+=W^+\\). The invariants are restored.</p><p>After all rounds, every pair of labels is adjacent. The \\(t\\) disjoint connected bags form a \\(K_t\\) model, contradicting \\(h(G)&lt;t\\). This proves separability.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Theorem 8.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are absolute positive integers \\(K,Q\\) such that every \\(Ka\\)-connected graph \\(P\\) with \\( \\ell(P)\\ge Qa \\) is \\((a,3a)\\)-woven.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Wovenness is defined in Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(a\\ge1\\) is an integer.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\) is a finite graph.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are absolute positive integers \\(K,Q\\) such that every \\(Ka\\)-connected graph \\(P\\) with\n\\[\n\\ell(P)\\ge Qa\n\\]\nis \\((a,3a)\\)-woven.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix all constants from earlier propositions. Choose an integer \\(K\\ge100\\) such that\n\\[\nK-14&gt;128D_{K_0}. \\tag{46}\n\\]\nChoose an absolute integer \\(a_0\\ge24\\) so large that all sufficiently-large-parameter conclusions used below hold for \\(t=64a\\) whenever \\(a&gt;a_0\\), and also\n\\[\na'=2\\lceil a/3\\rceil\\le3a/4 \\quad(a&gt;a_0), \\tag{47}\n\\]\n\\[\nt^{1.06}+7a\\le t^{11/10}\\quad(t=64a,\\ a&gt;a_0).\n\\]\nThese choices do not involve \\(Q\\). Finally choose an integer \\(Q\\) satisfying\n\\[\nQ/4&gt;\n10+64(c_{42}+6K_0+2S)+(9/2)K, \\tag{48}\n\\]\n\\[\nQ&gt;10+64(c_{42}+6K_0+3S), \\tag{49}\n\\]\n\\[\nQ&gt;128C_*\\log(128a_0)+1. \\tag{50}\n\\]</p><p>Proceed by strong induction on \\(a\\). Fix \\(a\\) roots and at most \\(3a\\) admissible pairs. Let \\(Z_0\\) be their union, so \\(|Z_0|\\le7a\\). Give each root and each end of every noncoincident pair a neighboring proxy outside \\(Z_0\\), all proxies distinct, including different roles of the same vertex. Greedy choice is possible because \\(\\delta(P)\\ge Ka\\) and fewer than \\(14a\\) vertices are forbidden. Coincident pairs need no proxies.</p><p>Put \\(G=P-Z_0\\), and let \\(Z\\) be the proxy set. Then\n\\[\na\\le|Z|\\le7a,\\qquad\nG\\text{ is }(K-7)a\\text{-connected},\\qquad\n\\ell(G)\\ge Qa-7a. \\tag{51}\n\\]\nIt suffices to construct in \\(G\\) a clique model rooted at the root proxies and paths for the other proxy pairs, all disjoint. Adding original vertices and proxy edges restores exactly the permitted root–path coincidences. A coincident pair at a root meets only that root’s expanded bag.</p><p>Set \\(t=64a\\). If \\(h(P)\\ge t\\), delete from a \\(K_t\\) model every bag meeting \\(Z_0\\). A \\(K_{57a}\\) model remains in \\(G\\). Since\n\\[\n57a\\ge4|Z|,\\qquad (K-7)a\\ge2|Z|,\n\\]\nProposition 6 gives a clique model rooted at every proxy. Retain the root-proxy bags. For each other pair, join its proxies through their two bags and an edge between them. All such paths and retained bags are disjoint. This proves the large-minor case for every \\(a\\).</p><p>Now suppose \\(h(P)&lt;64a\\). Proposition 5 gives\n\\[\n\\ell(P)\\le128C_*a\\log(128a)+1.\n\\]\nFor \\(1\\le a\\le a_0\\), this is strictly less than \\(Qa\\) by (50), so the minor-free case is impossible in the base range. Assume \\(a&gt;a_0\\).</p><p>Every vertex of \\(G-Z\\) has degree at least \\((K-14)a&gt;2D_{K_0}t\\). Thus \\(G-Z\\) is nonempty and has density greater than \\(D_{K_0}t\\). Proposition 5 gives a \\(K_0t\\)-connected district\n\\[\nD\\subseteq G-Z,\\qquad |V(D)|\\le t^{1.06}.\n\\]</p><p>Proposition 6 supplies two paths per proxy from \\(Z\\) to distinct vertices of \\(D\\), sharing only repeated starts. Choose such a system \\(\\mathcal Q_1\\) of minimum total length in \\(G\\). It has at most \\(14a\\) paths. Put\n\\[\nX=Z\\cup V(D),\\qquad\nY=V(\\mathcal Q_1)\\setminus X.\n\\]\nBy the choice of \\(a_0\\), \\(|X|\\le t^{11/10}\\). Proposition 8 gives \\(28a\\)-degeneracy of \\(G[Y]\\), and at most \\(42a\\) neighbors in \\(Y\\) for a vertex outside \\(X\\cup Y\\). These bounds are at most \\(42t\\), so Proposition 4 gives\n\\[\n\\ell(G-X-Y)\\ge Qa-7a-c_{42}t. \\tag{52}\n\\]\nBy (49), this exceeds \\(6K_0t\\). Proposition 3 gives an induced \\(K_0t\\)-connected\n\\[\nH'\\subseteq G-X-Y\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\ell(H')\\ge Qa-7a-(c_{42}+6K_0)t. \\tag{53}\n\\]</p><p>In \\(G-Z\\), the disjoint sets \\(H',D\\) each have more than \\(K_0t\\) vertices, and connectivity is at least \\((K-14)a&gt;2a\\). Proposition 6 gives \\(2a\\) disjoint paths between them. Truncate at the last visit to \\(H'\\) and first subsequent visit to \\(D\\), so internal vertices avoid both sets. These paths avoid \\(Z\\).</p><p>Proposition 8 combines these paths with \\(\\mathcal Q_1\\) into disjoint paths to \\(D\\) from all proxies and from a set \\(W\\subseteq V(H')\\), \\(|W|=a\\), meeting \\(H',D\\) only at appropriate ends.</p><p>It is enough to construct a clique model rooted at \\(W\\) entirely inside \\(H'\\). Indeed, in \\(D\\), group the terminal ends by pairing each root proxy with a different member of \\(W\\), and pair all other proxies as prescribed. There are at most \\(|Z|+a\\le8a\\) terminals, and \\(K_0t\\ge8\\gamma a\\), so Proposition 1 supplies disjoint grouping sets. These and the outside paths attach the internal rooted model to the root proxies and complete the pair paths. The outside paths meet \\(H'\\) only in \\(W\\).</p><p>For each \\(w\\in W\\), choose two neighbors in \\(H'-W\\), all \\(2a\\) choices distinct. Minimum degree \\(K_0t\\) permits this. Let \\(N\\) be these neighbors and put\n\\[\nH''=H'-W-N.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\ell(H'')\\ge r_0:=\nQa-10a-(c_{42}+6K_0)t. \\tag{54}\n\\]\nBy (49), \\(r_0&gt;3St\\). Apply Proposition 10 to \\(H''\\), and then to one resulting subgraph. The first application is legal since \\(\\ell(H'')\\ge r_0&gt;2St\\); each output has list chromatic number at least \\(r_0-St&gt;2St\\), so the second is legal. We obtain three disjoint subgraphs, each with list chromatic number at least \\(r_0-2St\\).</p><p>Let \\(a'=2\\lceil a/3\\rceil\\), and put\n\\[\nA=10+64(c_{42}+6K_0+2S).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nr_0-2St-6Ka'-Qa'\n=Q(a-a')-Aa-6Ka'\n\\]\n\\[\n\\ge\\bigl(Q/4-A-(9/2)K\\bigr)a&gt;0\n\\]\nby (47) and (48). In particular \\(r_0-2St&gt;6Ka'\\). Proposition 3 supplies, within the three subgraphs, disjoint induced \\(Ka'\\)-connected districts \\(J_1,J_2,J_3\\), each with list chromatic number at least \\(Qa'\\). Since \\(a'&lt;a\\), induction makes each \\((a',3a')\\)-woven.</p><p>Partition the labels of \\(W\\) into three nonempty blocks \\(B_1,B_2,B_3\\), each of size at most \\(\\lceil a/3\\rceil\\). Assign one district to every pair of blocks, naming them \\(J_{12},J_{13},J_{23}\\). In each district designate distinct roots for the labels of its two blocks, and add distinct padding roots to bring the total to \\(a'\\). The district has enough vertices. Each label has two designated roots, in different districts, and every pair of labels occurs together in a district, including labels in the same block.</p><p>Within \\(H'-W\\), join the two selected neighbors of each \\(w\\) to its two designated roots, respectively, by disjoint paths. This uses Proposition 1 on \\(4a\\) distinct terminals: neighbors lie in \\(N\\), while districts lie in \\(H''\\), and\n\\[\nK_0t-a\\ge4\\gamma a.\n\\]\nThere are \\(2a\\) paths.</p><p>Apply Proposition 7 successively in the three districts to construct the child rooted models while rerouting the paths and retaining their ends. Each district has enough capacity because\n\\[\n2a\\le3a'=6\\lceil a/3\\rceil.\n\\]\nDiscard padding bags. Every designated root remains its path endpoint, so retained bags meet paths only at their own designated roots. Later rerouting in disjoint districts creates no new intersection with previously retained bags.</p><p>For each \\(w\\), unite its two designated bags, their respective paths, the two edges to \\(w\\), and \\(w\\). These \\(a\\) sets are connected, disjoint, and rooted at \\(W\\). Every two have an edge because their labels occur together in a child clique model. This constructs the rooted \\(K_a\\) model inside \\(H'\\), and completes the induction.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 6, "cite"], [2, 7, "cite"], [2, 8, "cite"], [2, 9, "cite"], [2, 10, "cite"], [2, 11, "cite"], [2, 26, "cite"], [2, 28, "cite"], [2, 33, "cite"], [2, 34, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [5, 2, "cite"], [6, 15, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [7, 9, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [9, 11, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [11, 6, "cite"], [12, 0, "cite"], [12, 15, "cite"], [12, 32, "cite"], [12, 33, "cite"], [12, 34, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 12, "cite"], [15, 32, "cite"], [15, 33, "cite"], [15, 34, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [17, 28, "cite"], [17, 32, "cite"], [17, 33, "cite"], [17, 34, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [18, 21, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [19, 21, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [21, 17, "cite"], [22, 31, "cite"], [22, 33, "cite"], [22, 34, "cite"], [23, 22, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [23, 25, "cite"], [24, 22, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 22, "cite"], [26, 33, "cite"], [26, 34, "cite"], [27, 26, "cite"], [28, 26, "cite"], [29, 32, "cite"], [29, 33, "cite"], [29, 34, "cite"], [30, 29, "cite"], [31, 29, "cite"], [32, 33, "cite"], [33, 34, "cite"], [34, 0, "cite"]]});
